七.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – a vertical stroke and a horizontal stroke bisecting each other – original character of {{och-l|切|to cut}}.<ref>https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E4%B8%83</ref>

The top-most horizontal stroke is done from left to right. The vertical stroke and bottom-most horizontal stroke are done as one stroke, from top-left to bottom-right.

三.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|i}} - three parallel strokes. Compare with {{zh-l|*一}}, one stroke meaning “one”, {{zh-l|*二}}, two strokes meaning “two”, and {{zh-l|*亖}}, four strokes meaning “four”.
{{zh-etym-triple|一|one}}. See also [[畺]]. Unrelated to [[丰]].

不.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}

{{liushu|p}}: the [[calyx]] of a flower. {{lang|zh|不}} was composed into a phono-semantic character with the pictograph for mouth ({{zh-l|*口}}), to form {{och-l|否}}, representing “no” (negation). This composed meaning then spread back to the original character {{lang|zh|不}}, making it a synonym of {{lang|zh|否}}. A new character of {{och-l|柎}} was eventually created to represent the original meaning of calyx.

Following ''{{w|Shuowen}}''’s interpretation, [[w:Bernhard Karlgren|Karlgren]] and [[w:Léon Wieger|Wieger]] interpret it as a bird flying toward the sky ({{zh-l|*一}}). The sky being the limit for the bird, thus the idea of negation.

世.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|j}}: Variant form of {{och-l|*枼}} (the original form of {{och-l|葉|leaf|id=2}}) with the tree removed.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' interprets it as {{och-l|*十}} triplicated: thirty [[year]]s make one [[generation]] (thirty times twelve months gives 360 months).

乏.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Chen Jian suggests that the graph derives from {{zh-l|⿱口⿰止止}}, an ideogram shared by {{zh-l|*範}} and {{zh-l|*圍}}: one {{zh-l|*止}} was omitted, and one stroke was slanted to avoid confusion with {{zh-l|*正}}.<ref>陳劍（2020）。「尋『詞』推『字』」之一例：試說殷墟甲骨文中「犯」「圍」兩讀之字。中國文字，(總第四)，71-115。https://www.airitilibrary.com/Article/Detail?DocID=P20241129001-N202501150005-00005
</ref>

九.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – a stylized hand, with bent wrist/forearm (hence the hook stroke at lower right). Earlier forms resemble {{l|zh|手}}, {{l|zh|寸}}. The original meaning of the glyph was “elbow”, which is now written {{och-l|肘}}.

After the meaning “elbow” was forgotten, {{lang|zh|九}} was taken to symbolize a [[fist]] tightening to [[bump up]] against something; thus, there is a metaphorical [[bumping]] up of [[nine]] against [[ten]], which is the last number when counting on one's fingers.

乞.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Variant of {{och-l|气}}, distinguished to indicate a phonetically borrowed meaning “to beg”.

The top component of the character is unrelated to {{zh-l|*旅}}, {{zh-l|*傷}}, {{zh-l|*复}}, {{zh-l|*每}} as well as {{zh-l|*人}}.

京.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – a tall building. Compare {{zh-l|*高}}.

When used as a component on top of the character, the character loses the bottom part {{zh-l|*小}}. (e.g., {{zh-l|*亮}} and {{zh-l|*亭}}).

今.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|i}} – An inverted {{l|zh|曰}} ("to speak"), hence [[亼]] with a horizontal stroke at the bottom, so the inverted mouth means "not to speak".

Another theory suggests that it depicts something in the mouth. Maybe the original form of {{och-l|噤}} or {{och-l|吟}}. If so, the derivative {{och-l|含}} refers to the original word.

他.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|人|也|c1=s|c2=p|alt1=亻|t1=man|ls=psc}}.

Corrupted form of {{och-l|佗}}, which is found in {{w|Zuozhuan}}.

仙.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|人|alt1=亻|山|ls=ic|ls2=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=person|t2=mountain}} — a person moving into a mountain to practise becoming immortal.

Originally {{zh-l|*僊}}. The current form is first attested in the clerical script of the Han dynasty.

以.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – a person ({{zh-l|*人}}) carrying something. It is often simplified to {{zh-l|*㠯}} in pre-[[Qin]] ancient scripts. Today, the person is partly visible on the right (see also the stylization in [[从]]).

See also [[重]], in which a person is carrying a bag, [[央]], in which a person is carrying a pole, and [[年]], in which a person is carrying the harvest.

任.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|人|alt1=亻|t1=person|壬|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}} – to carry on a shoulder pole.

Note that its phonetic part {{och-l|壬|id=2}} may be the graphical representation of {{lang|zh|任}}, depicting a shoulder pole rotated by 90 degrees.

便.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
According to ''{{w|Shuowen}}'', an {{Han compound|人|更|alt1=亻|ls=ic|t1=person|t2=to change|nocap=y}} – to change one's posture to make oneself comfortable.

Alternatively, this may be the original character for {{och-l|鞭|to whip}}, from {{Han compound|人|𠓥|t1=person|t2=whip}}, as seen in the bronze script.

係.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|人|系|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc}}.

The simplified form {{zh-l|*系}} also exists as a traditional character, and is also used to simplify {{zh-l|*繫}}.  In ancient times, {{zh-l|系|tr=-}}, {{zh-l|係*}}, and {{zh-l|繫*}} were usually interchangeable graphic variants, although in some compounds, only one form is acceptable (e.g. {{zh-l|世系|tr=-}} and {{zh-l|繋辭|tr=-}}).
{{rfref|en}}
The meaning “yes” in Cantonese and Hakka is likely an independently created {{sound-symbolic|zh|title=sound symbolism}}, only associated with this character by {{glossary|folk etymology}}. {{etystub|zh}}

保.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
In the oracle script, this character was an {{Han compound|人|子|ls=ic|t1=person|t2=child|nocap=1}} – a person carrying a child on his back; to carry (a child) > to protect. See also [[好]].

In early bronze inscriptions, {{zh-l|*爪|hand}} was added to the person to emphasize that the person was carrying the child. This was later simplified into one or two strokes. The current form has two.

偽.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han etym|僞|no_img=y}}
{{Han simp|僞|f=爲|t=為}}. {{Han compound|人|alt1=亻|為|t1=man|t2=to make|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}, originally “artificial”.

傷.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|人|𥏻|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

Current form resembles {{Han compound|从|昜}}. The top component of the character is unrelated to {{zh-l|*旅}}, {{zh-l|*每}}, {{zh-l|*复}}, {{zh-l|*乞}} as well as {{zh-l|*人}}.

光.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
[[File:Sanxingdui Bronze Altar Component Resembling Character Guang (光).jpg|thumb|class=skin-invert-image|A Late Shang Dynasty bronze altar component from Sanxingdui.]]
{{Han compound|火|t1=[[fire]]|卩|t2=[[kneel]]ing [[person]]|ls=ic}} – fire over a person's [[head]], emphasizing the shining of light (''{{w|Shuowen}}'').

克.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Origin uncertain. Oracle bone and bronze forms consist of {{zh-l|*由}} (or simply {{zh-l|*口}}) over a sideways-standing basin (see also {{zh-l|*般}}).

The sideways basin may signify overturning, extending to mean "to change"—representing the word {{och-l|*革|tr=*kˤrək}}.

免.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – a man wearing a ceremonial hat. Then borrowed for abstract meanings. The derivative {{och-l|冕}} refers to the original word.

See also [[敖]], [[冒]], and [[美]].

入.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|i}} - an arrowhead indicating "to enter".

Compare {{zh-l|人}}.

八.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}}: two bent lines indicating the original meaning of "to divide". This character is later borrowed to mean "eight" because of homonymy, making the original meaning obsolete (now represented by {{zh-l|*分}} and {{zh-l|*別}}).

Unrelated to the bottom part of [[兵]], [[共]], [[具]], and [[興]], in which it represents two hands holding an object. Also unrelated to [[只]] and [[兑]] in which it represents air coming out of a mouth. Unrelated to [[貝]]. Eventually, unrelated to the bottom part of [[其]], [[真]], and [[典]], in which it represents the legs of a table.

共.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|廾|口|t1=two hands|t2=an object|ls=ic}} – holding up an object with two hands. Eventually {{zh-l|*口}} (not {{m-g|mouth}}) came to be written {{zh-l|*廿}} (not {{m-g|twenty}}), which later fused with {{zh-l|*廾}} to form the modern 共.

Possibly the original form of {{zh-l|*供|to provide; to offer}}.

具.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|鼎|t1=cauldron|廾|t2=two hands|ls=ic}} – to prepare food. A semi-conservative variant [[𥃲]] is similar but unrelated to [[算]].

{{Shuowen}} mistakes the middle component to be {{och-l|*貝}} and interprets the character as referring to currency.

典.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|冊|t1=books|丌|t2=table|ls=ic}} – (official) books on a table.

Unrelated to {{zh-l|*曲}} and [[曹]].

冊.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – {{w|bamboo slips}} strung together. See also [[侖]], [[扁]], and [[典]]. Similar but unrelated to [[龠]]. The contemporary stylized form [[册]] is unrelated to [[朋]].
[[File:Absence documents on bamboo slips scroll in Han Dynasty 01.jpg|thumb|冊: bamboo slips strung together]]

冒.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|冃|t1=hat|目|t2=eyes|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}. {{och-l|*冃}} is the original form, {{och-l|*目}} was added later. Original form of {{och-l|帽|hat}}.

See also [[敖]], [[免]], and [[美]].

冠.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|冖|元|寸|ls=psc|ls2=ic|c1=s|c2=p|c3=s|t1=hat|t2=head|t3=hand}} – a hand putting a hat on the head.

Earlier forms are {{Han compound|冃|元|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=hat|nocap=1|adj=y}}.

冥.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally {{Han compound|冖|日|廾|t1=cloth cover|t2=sun|t3=two hands|ls=ic|nocap=y}} – "dark". The upper part of the character is 𠕾. The {{lang|zh|廾}} was later corrupted to {{zh-l|*六}}.

May also be a {{Han compound|冖|日|廾|c1=p|c2=s|c3=s|ls=psc|nocap=1}} (''{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}'').

冶.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{Han compound|ls=ic|呂|t1=metal ingots|刀|t2=ladle|口|t3=a decorative component}} — to cast metal.

The ingots have simplified into {{och-l|*冫}} (compare {{och-l|*金}} where they appear as {{och-l|*丷}}) and the {{och-l|*刀}} and {{och-l|*口}} have combined together, forming the phonetic component {{och-l|台}}.<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref>

出.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{Han compound|止|凵|ls=ic|t1=foot|t2=cave}} – to step out of a cave; to exit. Compare {{och-l|*各}}.

By the {{w|Qin dynasty}}, the character has lost its original shape. Based on the distorted form, ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' mistakenly interprets the character as a pictogram of a plant ({{och-l|*屮}}) growing outwards.

列.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|歹|刀|t1=skeletal remains|t2=knife|ls=ic}} – to tear apart.

The seal script form can be analyzed as a {{Han compound|𡿪|刀|c1=p|c2=s|t2=knife|ls=psc|nocap=y}}.

初.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|衣|刀|alt1=衤|ls=ic|t1=clothes|t2=knife; to cut}} – start making clothes by cutting the cloth.

The various {{l|zh|𢀰}}-shaped glyphs are or are evolved from {{w|Zetian characters}}.

則.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|alt2=刂|鼎|刀|ls=ic|t1=[[ding]], a bronze cauldron for cooking or sacrificial rituals|t2=knife}}; hence, the original version is [[𠟭]]. Perhaps it's a reference to carving something (e.g., characters) with a knife on a bronze. Its original meaning was "rule; law".

鼎 was simplified to the unrelated 貝 in the small seal script. Compare {{och-l|貞|gloss=to divine; righteous; firm}} (< [[鼑]]), which underwent the same stylization.

加.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
''{{w|Shuowen}}'': {{Han compound|力|口|t1=strength|t2=mouth|ls=ic|nocap=1}} — exaggerate; slander. 

Sun Changxu: {{Han compound|力|口|t1=plowing|t2=mouth|ls=ic|nocap=1}} — to put more effort. 口 here indicates exclamations or breath associated with the effort.

勝.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|朕|力|c1=p|c2=s|t2=strength|ls=psc}}.

Graphically, the character decomposes as {{Han compound|月|劵}} (which gives rise to the simplified form {{zh-l|*胜}}), though this is not the etymology.

化.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|ic}} of two {{zh-l|*人|person}}, one upright ({{zh-l|*亻}}) and one upside down ({{zh-l|*𠤎}}) — reversal; change. ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' also considers {{zh-l|*𠤎}} to be a phonetic component.

Compare [[并]], [[比]], [[卬]], [[尼]] and [[北]].

北.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|ic}} – two men back to back. Originally meaning “back”; the character {{och-l|背}} refers to the original word.

Compare [[化]], [[比]], [[卬]], and [[尼]].

匠.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Traditionally thought to be an {{Han compound|匚|斤|ls=ic|t1=box|t2=axe|nocap=1}} (e.g. ''{{w|Shuowen}}'').

In more recent scholarship (e.g. {{zh-ref|Zhengzhang, 2003}}; {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart, 2014}}), an alternative glyph origin has been proposed, suggesting that it is a {{Han compound|匚|斤|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=axe|nocap=1}}.

匹.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

Uncertain. Various theories have been proposed based on the evidence from the bronze inscriptions:
* {{liushu|p}} of folds in cloth ({{zh-ref|Lin, 1920}});
* {{Han compound|石|乙|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=rock}} or {{ltc-l|乀}}. The phonetic component may be replaced with {{och-l|匕|id=1}} (not shown above);
* {{liushu|i}}: half of {{zh-l|*丙|two horses}} – one horse ({{zh-ref|Zhang et al., 1996}}), but the origin of the character {{lang|zh|丙}} is also uncertain (for example, it could be the tail of a fish).

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' considers it to be an {{Han compound|八|匸|ls=ic|ls2=psc|t1=eight|t2=to conceal|c1=p|c2=s|nocap=1}} – unit of measuring cloth. {{zh-l|*八}} refers to eight folds in one {{zh-l|*匹}} of cloth (two bolts of cloth), and {{zh-l|*匸}} refers to concealing the two ends of the cloth when rolling it up. This interpretation is not likely since the older forms do not evidently have these components.

千.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|一|人|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc|t1=one, signifying a number}}. The Old Chinese pronunciations of {{och-l|千}} and {{och-l|人}} were similar. For the component {{zh-l|*人}}, compare its combining form {{zh-l|*亻}}.

The traditional explanation holds that the extra line indicates an [[extension]] (see the etymologies of {{zh-l|*年}} and {{zh-l|*延}}). {{lang|zh|千}} has the meaning ''[[one thousand]]'' because ''one thousand'' is a number that is reached by extending one's counting.

升.originRaw:
[[File:两诏铜方升.jpg|thumb|[[Qin]]-era bronze standard dipper measuring one {{m-self|zh|升}} (''sheng'')]]
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} of a dipper, compare {{l|mul|斗}}.

卒.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
There were 5 forms of {{lang|zh|卒}} in Shang oracle bone script ({{zh-ref|Qiu, 1990}}; {{zh-ref|Xuan, 2012}}):
# [[File:卒-oracle-2.svg|30px]]: same as {{zh-l|*衣|clothes}} (> Western Zhou bronze script [[File:卒-bronze.svg|30px]]);
# [[File:卒-oracle-3.svg|30px]]: a crisscross {{zh-l|*衣}} - a completed clothing;
# [[File:卒-oracle.svg|30px]] ({{lang|zh|卒}}): {{liushu|ic|nocap=y}}: {{zh-l|*衣|clothes}} with a mark - a completed clothing to be folded (> Warring States bronze script [[File:卒-bronze-warring.svg|30px]]; Chu slip and silk script [[File:卒-silk.svg|30px]]);
# [[File:𧙻-oracle.svg|30px]] ({{zh-l|*𧙻}}): {{liushu|psc|nocap=y}}: {{zh-l|*衣|clothes}} + {{och-l|聿}} (hand ({{zh-l|*又}}) holding a brush);
# [[File:𧙻-oracle-3.svg|30px]],[[File:𧙻-oracle-2.svg|30px]]: {{lang|zh|𧙻}} having {{lang|zh|又}} omitted.
The brush shape in form (5) was often placed upside down. Later the inverted brush shape was mistaken as {{lang|zh|又}} and then moved to the top as in Chu slip and silk script [[File:𰠄-silk.svg|30px]] ({{zh-l|*𰠄}}) to avoid obscuration with {{zh-l|*袁}} ({{lang|zh|衣}} + {{lang|zh|又}}) ({{zh-ref|Xuan, 2012}}).

印.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|爪|卩|ls=ic|t1=hand|t2=kneeling person}} – a hand suppressing a kneeling person.

According to 羅振玉 in his book 《增訂殷墟書契考釋》(1915), the character is an ideogram of a hand suppressing a kneeling person. The character {{l|zh|抑}} (to suppress) was used to preserve the original meaning.

危.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|厃|卩|t1=person on a cliff or mountain|t2=kneeling person|ls=ic}}.

Written like {{zh-l|*𡴲}} during the {{w|Warring States period}}, an {{Han compound|人|山|t1=person|t2=mountain|ls=ic|nocap=y}}.

又.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – a right hand. Original form of {{och-l|右|right}}. Unrelated to [[𠬤]] (> [[译]]).

Often written as [[⺕]] in many modern characters such as [[事]], [[聿]], [[雪]], [[急]], [[爭]], [[尹]], and [[建]] (which is unrelated to 聿).

友.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|又|又|c1=p|c2=p|ls=psc}}<ref>季旭昇撰 『説文新証』 芸文印書館、2014年、210-211頁。<br>徐超 『古漢字通解500例』 中華書局、2022年、243頁。</ref>

Shuowen: two hands, therefore friendship.

叔.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|尗|又|ls=ic|ls2=psc|t1=wooden stake|t2=hand|c1=p|c2=s}} – using a wooden stake to dig the ground. Later phonetically borrowed to mean "paternal uncle".

The whole character is partly related to [[建]] since it represents a hand planting a wooden stake in the ground.

取.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|耳|t1=ear|又|t2=hand|ls=ic}} - to take the ear of fallen prey, a rite of hunters mentioned in the Rites of Zhou.

{{zh-x|大 獸 公 之，小 禽 私 之，獲 者 取 左 耳。|collapsed=y|ref=Zhouli}}

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' interprets it as taking the ear of an enemy instead. <!--(《又部》取：捕取也。从又从耳。《周禮》：「獲者取左耳。」《司馬法》曰：「載獻聝。」聝者，耳也。).--> However, Wang (2022) points out that there is no evidence that this actually happened in war.<ref>[https://www.fdgwz.org.cn/Web/Show/10934 王鵬遠 說“聝”]</ref>

口.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – resembles an open mouth.

In the oracle bone script, radical {{lang|zh|口}} is used for characters having abstract meanings. Such a character can have a figurative meaning derived from the phonetic part, e.g. {{och-l|右|gloss=right}} = {{och-l|又|gloss=right hand}} + {{lang|zh|口}} and {{och-l|𪪺|gloss=strong; powerful}} = {{och-l|弓|gloss=bow}} + {{lang|zh|口}}, or be an unrelated borrowing, e.g. {{och-l|否|gloss=no}} = {{och-l|不|gloss=calyx}} + {{lang|zh|口}}.

古.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|i}}: A shield ({{och-l|*甲}}, contraction of [[盾]] and similar but unrelated to [[中]]) and the radical of the mouth ({{och-l|*口}}) both as a distinguishing mark and as a component that partly indicates pronunciation. Together, they indicate the concept of "strong, firm" (as strong as a shield); original form of {{och-l|固}} (Qiu Xigui, Ding Shan 丁山). Compare oracle bone forms with {{zh-l|*干}}, {{zh-l|*盾}}. Unrelated to [[中]] and hence to [[史]] and [[使]].

{{zh-ref|Fang (1982)}} finds evidence for the 十 component originally being a depiction of a piece of food and the character originally being a depiction of someone spitting out a piece of food due to his bad taste. Thus, Fang argues this character is the original form of {{zh-l|苦}}, "bitter".

句.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|丩|口|c1=s|c2=p|t1=to entangle|ls=psc}}, which may represent the tip of a big nail.

The {{zh-l|*丩}} component, originally two entangled ropes, now resembles the unrelated {{zh-l|*勹}}.

召.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|刀|口|c1=p|c2=s|t2=mouth|ls=psc}} – originally, to call out (with one’s mouth) or to serve wine for guests. Part of the second original meaning is preserved in [[招]].

In the most ancient versions on some oracle bones, the character was totally different: it represented one or two hands over a wine vessel; the knife and mouth components were added later to this variant.

台.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|㠯|口|c1=p|c2=s|t2=mouth|ls=psc}}.

An alternate variant of {{och-l|㠯}} (modern form: {{m|zh|以}}) in [[w:Chinese bronze inscriptions|Chinese bronze inscriptions]].

史.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{liushu|p}} – {{och-l|又|hand}} holding a stylized flag almost identical to {{och-l|中}} perhaps to indicate a representative or ambassador (see its variant [[吏]] used in [[使]] to mean "ambassador"). As an alternative, it holds either a pen or a hunting weapon resembling similar to 中. Hence, the inner component 中 is partly unclear.

The whole character originally indicates the act of taking notes; today, it means "history". Compare [[事]].

同.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
[[File:Illustration of Tong 同.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Illustration of Gu 觚]]
{{liushu|p}}: a pipe — original form of {{och-l|筒|tr=*lˤoŋ}}.

名.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|夕|t1=crescent moon|口|t2=mouth|ls=ic}} — to say one's own name to identify oneself in the dark.

An alternative theory has been proposed based on Shang era texts. [[口]] was originally used to write both [[口]] and [[名]]. [[夕]] standing for what later came to be written [[明]], was added as a phonetic indicator to disambiguate the two meanings.

吏.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Alternative descendant of {{m|zh|叓|tr=-}}, which is the ancient form of {{m|zh|事|tr=-}}. Furthermore, both characters are related to [[史]], which was born as a variant without the horizontal stroke on top.

The character should represent a hand holding a flag to mean "representative, ambassador"; see [[使]].

周.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

Oracle bone script: {{liushu|p}}: a jade artifact with carved patterns. Original form of {{och-l|彫|to carve}}. The character was later borrowed as a country name and {{och-l|*口}} was added as a distinguishing mark. A semi-conservative version in which the carvings are visible is {{och-l|𠕛}}. The top part of the character gradually became similar to {{och-l|*用}} in the late Western Zhou period.<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref> See also {{och-l|*黃}}, which could have a reference to a jade artifact.

Folk etymology interprets the seal script form as {{och-l|*口|mouth}} + {{och-l|*用|to use}}, indicating the idea of using words skillfully to express oneself, thereby implying the meaning of being meticulous or detailed.<ref>Digital Shinjigen 2017</ref>

四.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}}: a nose exhaling. Original form of {{och-l|呬}}. Later {{Han phonetically borrowed}} to represent {{och-l|四|four}}.<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref>

The word "four" was written as {{och-l|*亖}} before {{w|Western Zhou}} and {{och-l|*四}} appeared in late {{w|Spring and Autumn period}}. This alternative form was used to prevent confusion of {{och-l|*亖}} and {{l|zh|二|tr=-|t=two}} or {{l|zh|三|tr=-|t=three}} in vertical writing. It was standardized in {{w|Qin dynasty}}.

因.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|囗|大|t1=sheet|t2=person|ls=ic}} – man on a mattress. Original form of {{och-l|裀|mat; layered garment}} or {{och-l|茵|mattress; cushion}}.

Alternatively, possibly an {{liushu|ic|nocap=y}} of a man wearing clothing.

土.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Pictogram {{zh-l|象形}}: a mound of earth. In oracle-bone inscriptions, it could also refer to the soil deity, serving as the original graph of {{l|zh|社}}. The mound later became narrowed or pointed at both ends, with the broad middle part developing into the horizontal stroke.

Unrelated to the top component of [[夌]], [[走]], and [[周]]; also unrelated to the central component of [[金]] and the overall character [[圭]].

在.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|才|士|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=p}}.

In the oracle bone script and some of the bronze inscriptions, {{och-l|才}} is phonetically borrowed to represent this word; a phonetic {{och-l|士}} was added later. {{och-l|*士}} eventually corrupted into {{och-l|*土|earth}}.

埋.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

Originally written {{zh-l|薶}}, a {{Han compound|艸|alt1=艹|貍|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=plant|nocap=y}}. 

Later changed to 埋, a {{Han compound|土|里|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=earth|nocap=y}}.<ref>Digital Shinjigen 2017</ref>

塞.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Oracle bone script: {{Han compound|宀|工|廾|ls=ic|t1=house|t2=two items|t3=two hands}} – stuffing the house with items. Hence, the original version is [[𡨄]], a character similar but unrelated to [[共]] but partly related to [[展]].

''{{w|Shuowen}}'': {{Han compound|𡨄|土|ls=ic|t2=soil}}; the bottom component was added later.

外.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|卜|月|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

According to {{m|ja||学研漢和大字典|tr=Gakken Kanwa Dai Jiten}}, it is also an {{liushu|ic|nocap=1}}; {{zh-m|月|tr=-}} originally represents a crescent moon (i.e. the moon with part “missing” to the observer). The character is thus interpreted as representing divination based on the partial phase of the moon, implying the remaining visible part of the moon is the moon's ''‘outside’''. {{rfv-etym|zh}}

In oracle bone script, {{zh-m|*外}} was written identical to {{zh-m|*卜}},  but they were distinguished by the fact that {{zh-m|*卜}} has a horizontal bar extending to the inside of the tortoise shell, while {{zh-m|*外}} has a horizontal bar extending to the outside. {{rfv-etym|zh}}

多.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|ic}} – two pieces of meat ({{zh-l|*肉}}). In the bronze script, {{zh-l|*肉}} was corrupted into {{zh-l|*夕}} due to visual similarity, making {{zh-l|*多}} into a {{zh-etym-double|夕|nocap=y}}. The form with {{zh-l|*夕}} was inherited in later scripts.

{{zh-ref|Chi (2010)}} suggests that meat is scarce in ancient times, so two pieces of meat is a lot, citing a passage from ''{{w|Mencius (book)|Mencius}}'':
: {{zh-x|七十 者 可以 食 肉 矣。|Persons of seventy years may eat meat.|collapsed=y}}

Chang Ping-chuan suggests that it is the duplicative nature of the character that gives the meaning of "many", just like in {{zh-l|*林|forest}}, from {{zh-l|*木|tree; wood}}.

夜.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|亦|夕|t2=[[moon]]|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|abb1=y}}. {{och-l|夕}} may also serve as another phonetic component.

In the modern form, the phonetic {{l|zh|亦}} is distorted into {{Han compound|亠|亻|㇏}}, with the right-falling stroke {{l|zh|㇏}} merging into the semantic {{l|zh|夕}}.

太.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|psc}}: a differentiated form of {{och-l|大|big; great}} with a {{och-l|*丶}} added to represent {{och-l|太|too big}}.

Compare  {{och-l|*泰}}, which is also a differentiated form with {{och-l|*廾}} and {{och-l|*水}} added instead.

失.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}

{{liushu|p}} depicting a footprint of someone that has fallen surrounded by drops of blood ({{zh-ref|Ji Xusheng, 2004}}).

Alternatively, according to Shuowen, {{Han compound|手|乙|ls=ic}} — something falling from a hand ({{lang|zh|手}}). However, the original form doesn't show any hand.

奏.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|ic}}: two hands ({{zh-l|*廾}}) holding an object, perhaps a musical instrument used during a sacrifical ritual (the lower part of the object originally was similar to [[夲]]).

Otherwise, the whole character depicts two hands collecting resin ([[桼]]) dripping from a cut tree (drawn like [[屮]]). The resin is visible at the bottom of the character.

契.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|㓞|大|ls=psc|ls2=ic|c1=p|c2=s|t1=to engrave|t2=person}}, hence a person making an agreement and/or carving something with a knife.

Possibly a variant of {{zh-l|*栔}} or vice versa, given that the two characters were used interchangeably in ancient text.

奴.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|女|又|ls=ic|t1=woman|t2=hand}} – a hand capturing and ordering a woman around. {{och-l|女}} may also be phonetic.

Some oracle bone script forms (not depicted above) are {{liushu|p|adj=1|nocap=1}}, showing a person (probably a woman) with hands crossed at the back, as opposed to {{zh-l|*女}} which depicts a woman clasping her hands in front of the body.

妥.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|爪|女|t1=hand|t2=woman|ls=ic}} – to comfort, to placate. Original form of {{och-l|綏}}.

Compare {{zh-l|*孚}}.

婦.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|女|帚|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=woman}}.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' interprets it as a woman holding a broom.

婿.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|女|胥}}.

Attested in Qin materials as {{lang|zh|⿰士咠}}, which is of {{Han compound|c1=s|c2=p|士|咠}}; the phonetic part is later conflated into {{l|och|胥}} ({{zh-ref|Li, 2022}}).

宛.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|宀|夗|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

Its original meaning was perhaps linked to "house"; in ancient times, it was read as "[[館]]".

室.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|宀|至|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=roof}}. Its original meaning is "house, residence".

The arrows also indicates that a residence is a place or spot which somebody stops by, marked by an arrow on the ground, hence the phonetic component also adds part of the meaning. Therefore, the character can also be understood as an ideogrammic compound – "a ⼧ roof over ⾄ this exact spot". According to [[w:Shuowen Jiezi|Shuowen Jiezi]], it is indeed so, as the meaning is "至所止 arrive at a specific place, symbolized by the roof.

宰.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|宀|乂|t1=house|t2=to govern|ls=ic}} — house of government.<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref>

{{och-l|*乂}} originally appeared as similar to {{och-l|*䇂}} in the oracle bone script; in the modern form of this character, it has corrupted into {{och-l|*辛|}}.

容.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|宀|公|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}} – to hold; to contain.

容 has also largely supplanted {{och-l|頌}} for the sense of “appearance”.

寒.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|宀|人|茻|ls=ic|t1=house|t2=person|t3=grass}} – a person in a house, sleeping in the grass to protect from the cold. In some bronze inscriptions, {{zh-l|*二}} is added at the bottom of the character; this may simply be decorative or may represent a mat. In the seal script, {{zh-l|*二}} is changed to {{zh-l|*仌|ice}} to emphasize its meaning of coldness.

Contrast {{zh-l|*宿}}.

寡.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
In the {{w|bronze script}} from the {{w|Western Zhou period}}, it was originally an {{Han compound|ls=ic|nocap=1|宀|t1=house|頁|t2=head}} — only one person in the house; alone.

In the bronze script from the {{w|Warring States period}}, two decorative strokes were added around {{zh-l|*頁}}. The seal script found in ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' adds two additional strokes, making the component under {{zh-l|*宀}} appear as {{zh-l|*分}} and {{zh-l|*頁}}, leading ''Shuowen'' to erroneously interpret the character as an {{Han compound|ls=ic|nocap=1|宀|t1=house|頒|t2=to distribute}}.

寺.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|之|又|t1=to go|t2=hand|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|alt1=𡳿}} – to grasp, to hold. Phonetic 𡳿 ([[之]]), which represents a footprint pointing up, became {{zh-l|*土}} or {{zh-l|*士}} in the [[clerical script]] from the late Western Han to the Eastern Han, and semantic {{zh-l|*又}} became {{zh-l|*寸}} in Small Seal Script. The derivative {{och-l|持}} refers to the original word.

The character was often used in the place of a more specialized form. For example, in the {{w|Chu Silk Manuscript}} (see table above) it clearly stood for {{och-l|時|gloss=season}}.

封.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=psc|丰|土|寸|c1=p|c2=s|c3=s|t2=earth|t3=hand|abb1=yes}}. Compare {{zh-m|*邦}}.

Originally {{zh-m|*丰}}, depicting a tree seedling. Variants that added any combination of {{zh-m|*土|earth}}, {{zh-m|*又|hand}}, {{zh-m|*田|field}}, {{zh-m|*丮|kneeling person with hands stretched out}} appeared from bronze inscriptions onwards.

射.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
In the {{w|oracle bone script}}, an {{Han compound|弓|矢|t1=bow|t2=arrow|ls=ic|nocap=y}} – to shoot a bow. In the {{w|bronze inscriptions}}, a hand ({{zh-l|*又}}) drawing the bowstring was added. Hence, a conservative variant is [[𢎤]].

Later the bow and arrow on the left became {{zh-l|*身}} before or during the {{w|clerical script}} period, and the hand came to be written {{zh-l|*寸}}.

尊.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Shuowen: {{Han compound|酋|t1=wine|寸|t2=hand|ls=ic}} – a hand {{zh-l|寸}} offering a vessel of wine {{zh-l|酋}} to show respect and veneration.

However, based on the small seal script, it may instead be a variant of {{zh-l|𢍜}}, which is {{Han compound|酋|t1=wine|廾|t2=two hands|ls=ic}}.

尋.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|ic}}: two hands (now {{lang|zh|彐}} and {{lang|zh|寸}} ) stretched out measuring a mat (now {{lang|zh|工}}) – "a unit of measurement; [[arm span]]". The component {{zh-l|*口}} (“mouth, talk”) was likely added to emphasize the act of reading the measurement out loud. Forms with it have appeared as early as {{w|oracle bone script}}, but forms without it have also appeared as late as the {{w|Spring and Autumn period}}. 

Appears as {{zh-l|*𢒫}} in the Shuowen Jiezi, which interprets the character as {{liushu|psc|nocap=yes}} with {{och-l|彡}} as the sound component.

少.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – four dots, perhaps three grains of sand (see [[沙]]), to indicate "a few, a little". Phonetically borrowed from {{och-l|小|small}}, which is depicted by three dots.

Unrelated to [[步]] (and hence [[歲]]), in which the lower component is the stylization of a foot.

尻.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} in the oracle bone script – a person with the buttocks highlighted.

In the {{w|seal script}}, it developed into a {{Han compound|尸|九|ls=psc|t1=body|c1=s|c2=p|nocap=1}}.

局.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{Han compound|ls=psc|尸|句|c1=s|c2=p}}.

Folk etymology found in some dictionaries mistakes it for a representation of a deceased person's limbs being bent and buried.<ref>Digital Shinjigen 2017</ref>

屋.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|𡉉|室|c1=p|c2=s|t2=room|ls=psc}}. The upper part<!--https://zi.tools/zi/%E5%B1%8B--> has corrupted into the unrelated {{och-l|*尸}}.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' mistakes it for an ideogrammic compound.

展.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|尸|𧝑|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|abb2=y}}, which has a variant [[𧝣]]; the original version is [[㞡]]. The phonetic component is a reference to fine red silk clothes, one of the six garments worn by the queen in the Rites of Zhou, and it contains as well a reference to the concept of "opening, extending; watch carefully" in the component [[㠭]].

Similar but unrelated to [[昔]] and [[共]].

山.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – three mountain peaks. In the Oracle Bone Script, they were represented by triangles rather than vertical strokes. Compare {{zh-l|丘}}, particularly earlier forms.

Unrelated to [[出]] and the top component of [[岁]] and [[豐]]. Also unrelated to [[屮]] and [[巾]].

岩.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|山|石|ls=ic|t1=hill|t2=rock}}.

Originally written as {{zh-l|*巖}} – {{Han compound|山|嚴|c1=s|c2=p|t1=hill|ls=psc|nocap=1}}. The variant form {{lang|zh|岩}} first appeared in the clerical script in the Han dynasty era.

工.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – a bladed tool to plow the ground and to mark a border. As an alternative, a carpenter's square; according to the second interpretation, the character is related to the original meaning of [[巨]].

Unrelated to [[巠]] > [[𢀖]] and [[壬]].

左.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally written {{zh-l|*𠂇}} and [[䶹]] in the oracle bone script: {{liushu|p}} – a left hand. Compare {{zh-l|*又|right hand}}.

Late bronze inscription forms show an added {{zh-l|*工|work; to assist?}} component, making the current form a {{Han compound|𠂇|工|ls=psc|ls2=ic|c1=p|c2=s|t1=left hand|t2=work|nocap=1}}, representing the original form of {{och-l|佐|to assist}}.

巨.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|j}} of {{och-l|矩|carpenter's square}}, borrowed for {{och-l|巨|enormous}}. By the small seal script, the character was simplified and the man holding the carpenter's square was removed; however, the hand holding it appears to remain as the {{lang|zh|sc=Hani|コ}} shape inside the {{och-l|*工}}.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' interprets it as a pictogram, however Chen (2021) points out that {{och-l|*矩}} is the original shape.

差.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|來|𠂇|ls=ic|t1=wheat|t2=left hand}} – to [[hull]] [[wheat]] by rubbing grains inside hands. Original form of {{och-l|搓|to rub with hands; to scrub; to twist}}.

{{zh-l|*工}} or {{zh-l|*口}} was later added under {{lang|zh|𠂇}} to form {{och-l|左|left}}, which is also this character's phonetic component.

師.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|𠂤|帀|ls=ic|tr2=*tsˤəp}} .

{{och-l|師|tr=*sri}} may be related to words denoting multitude, abundance, or movement to and fro, such as {{och-l|詵|tr=*srən}}, {{och-l|駪|tr=*srən}}, {{och-l|甡|tr=*sriŋ}}, {{och-l|莘|tr=*srən}}, {{och-l|侁|tr=*sriŋ}}, {{och-l|瑟|tr=*srik}}, {{och-l|蝨|tr=*srik}} and {{och-l|蟋|tr=*srit}}.

常.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|尚|巾|c1=p|c2=s|t2=cloth|ls=psc}}.

The character originally referred to a "lower garment" similar to a skirt, before being phonetically borrowed to mean "long-lasting; frequently". The original sense is now represented by the character {{och-l|裳|lower garment}}.

年.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{Han compound|禾|人|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=wheat; grain}}. Originally meaning "harvest", it later came to mean "year". In the oracle bone script and early bronze inscriptions, it was originally written like {{och-l|*秂}}.

In bronze inscriptions after the {{w|Western Zhou}} period, a stroke was often added to {{och-l|*人}} to give {{och-l|千}}, which still acted as a phonetic component, thus getting ({{och-l|*秊}}). The current form is inherited from the clerical script, where ''{{w|libian}}'' ({{zh-l|*隸變}}) has occurred.

幾.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|𢆶|戍|ls=ic|t1=silk threads; little things|t2=to guard against}} – watching over the fine details.

Note that the simplified form {{och-l|几|small table}} is also a traditional character on its own.

庫.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|广|車|ls=psc|t1=building|c1=s|c2=p}}.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' incorrectly interprets it as an ideogrammic compound.

庶.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=psc|石|火|c1=p|c2=s|t1=stone|t2=fire}}. In the small seal script, {{lang|zh|石}} has corrupted into {{lang|zh|sc=Hani|𲟁}}. ''{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}'' interprets it as {{Han compound|广|炗|t1=roof|t2=light|nocap=y}}.

Compare {{och-l|度}} and {{och-l|席}}.

康.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|庚|米|t1=threshing tools|t2=rice|ls=ic}} – chaff. The clearest version is [[𢊪]], in which the two components can be identified well; in the semi-conservative variant [[𥹺]], the rice at the bottom is still well visible. The derivative {{zh-l|*糠}} refers to the original word.

Unrelated to [[隶]], in which the bottom part is a reference to a tail ([[毛]]); also unrelated to [[水]].

庸.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|庚|同|c1=s|c2=p|abb1=y|ls=psc|ls2=ic|tr2=*lˤoŋ}}.

Probably the original graph of {{zh-l|鏞}} (“large bell”), formed from {{zh-l|庚}} plus {{zh-l|同}} / {{zh-l|用}}.

廊.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|广|郎|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

{{zh-l|*良}} may be the original form of this character, see there for more.

延.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally {{zh-l|*㢟}}, an {{Han compound|ls=ic|彳|止|t1=walking|t2=foot|nocap=yes}} – "to travel far". The stroke at the top started being added as a distinguishing mark at the end of the {{w|Warring States period}}, as a way to distinguish its usage from {{lang|zh|㢟}} as an adverb to mean "prolonged", making it an {{liushu|i}}.<ref>季旭昇，2004《說文新證》，台北：藝文印書館印行，2014年9月第二版。p.137-138</ref><ref>李學勤) (2012) 《字源》, Tianjin: 天津古籍出版社。p.142-143</ref><ref>[https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%BB%B6 漢語多功能字庫 (Multi-function Chinese Character Database) - 字頭 「延」]</ref>

{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}} interprets it as {{Han compound|丿|㢟|c1=p|c2=s|ls=psc|nocap=yes}}.

建.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|廴|聿|ls=ic}}. Originally, a {{liushu|p|nocap=y}} — a hand (又) planting a pole (𰀁) into a base ({{zh-ref|Ji Xusheng, 2004}}). The left component was originally written as ∟. The right component is stylized as 聿; while the hand is still recognizable, the bottom part is unrelated to a writing brush.

The whole character is mostly similar to [[叔]].

引.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|弓|丿|ls=ic|t1=bow|t2=indicatory stroke}}

Oracle-bone and bronze forms depict a (often slack) bow with a short slant stroke added as an indicatory mark; it most likely marks the place/line of drawing the bow (i.e. pulling the string), hence the basic meaning “to pull; to draw (a bow)”. A newer view takes the stroke as pointing to the bow’s curved section (弓淵) and derives “to draw” secondarily, but this remains less certain {{zh-ref|(Sun 2025)}}.

弥.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|弓|尔|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc|t1=bow}}

Usage recorded as early the 6th century, found in the ''Yupian''{{lw|zh|pref=《|s:玉篇 (四庫全書本)/卷29|玉篇|suf=》}}, which was used interchangeably with {{zh-l|*彌}}.

弱.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|弓|彡|ls=ic|t1=bow|t2=hair, meaning "decoration" in this case}}—a decorative bow that is functionally inferior. Or it might be indicating that the bow is shaking, which implies a worn-out bow. 

An alternate etymology is 𢎥 + 𢎥, 𢎥 being a variant of 卷.

形.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|井|彡|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}.

Originally written as {{och-l|*刑}} in ancient texts. Based on the corrupted form of {{och-l|*井}}, ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' mistakes {{och-l|*幵}} to be the phonetic component. The variant form {{och-l|*𢒈}}, which preserves the original phonetic component, is found in tombstones from the {{w|Han dynasty|Han}} and {{w|Wei (state)|Wei}} times.<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref>

往.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally {{zh-m|*㞷/𫭠}}, a {{Han compound|止|王|c1=s|c2=p|t1=foot|ls=psc|nocap=y}}; see also the top component of {{zh-m|*出}}. Later {{zh-m|*彳}} was added to form {{zh-m|*𢓸}}, a {{Han compound|彳|止|王|c1=s|c2=s|c3=p|t1=to step|t2=foot|ls=psc|nocap=y}}. Eventually, the right component was stylized into the unrelated {{zh-m|*主}}. The footprint {{zh-m|*屮}} pointing upwards on top became a point as in the character {{zh-m|*之}}.

See also {{zh-l|*迋}}.

征.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|彳|正|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc|t2=straight}}. Also {{liushu|ic|nocap=1}}.

{{Han simp|徵|f=|t=正}} as well as existing already as a traditional character.

後.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
In oracle bone script, {{Han compound|ls=ic|幺|夊|t1=thread|t2=foot|nocap=yes}} – a foot bound by a rope, thus lagging behind. The component {{zh-l|*彳}} was added afterwards to emphasize this action.

Note that the simplified form {{och-l|*后}} is also a traditional character on its own, with a different meaning.

徹.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Oracle bone inscriptions: {{Han compound|鬲|又|ls=ic|t1=pot|t2=hand}} – a hand removing utensils after eating. Bronze inscriptions replaced {{zh-l|*又}} with {{zh-l|*攵}} to emphasize action.

In seal script {{zh-l|*彳}} was added to further emphasize action,  and what was originally a pot had corrupted into the unrelated {{zh-l|*育}}.

必.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
Likely a {{liushu|p|nocap=1}} – the [[handle]] ({{lang|zh|⿻⿺㇂丶丿}}) found on the right hand side of weapons such as {{och-l|*戊}} or {{och-l|*戈}} or {{och-l|*戉}} written stand-alone. Two decorative strokes were then added to the sides.

{{jiajie|柲|must}}

Unrelated to {{zh-l|*心}}, as bronze script makes clear, but in current form very similar.

志.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|之|心|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}.

Note that the top has been simplified and is now graphically {{zh-l|*士}}.

念.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}

Zhang (2021): {{Han compound|今|alt1=亼|心|ls=ic|t1=upside-down mouth|t2=heart}}: to listen to one's heart.<ref>張富海：《利用諧聲構擬上古音應該註意的幾個問題》，《出土文獻》，2021年第1期，頁134。)</ref> Original character for {{och-l|念|to think}}.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' interprets {{och-l|今}} to be a phonetic component, but the Old Chinese phonetics do not match.

思.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Original form was {{och-l|恖}}, considered by ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' to be a {{Han compound|ls=psc|囟|心|c1=p|c2=s|t2=heart|nocap=1}}. It may also be a {{Han compound|ls=ic|囟|心|t1=fontanel|t2=heart|nocap=1}}.

{{zh-l|*囟}} started to corrupt into the unrelated {{zh-l|*田}} as early as the silk script.

患.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|串|心|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=heart}}.

Since ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' does not have the character {{och-l|*串}}, it interprets the character as {{Han compound|吅|丨|心|c1=p|c2=s|c3=s|t3=heart}} – to pierce through something from the heart. {{w|Duan Yucai}} claims in his commentary that older copies of ''Shuowen'' actually have {{Han compound|毌|心|c1=p|c2=s|t1=to pierce through|t2=heart}} instead.

惜.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|心|alt1=忄|t1=heart|昔|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}} – a feeling (of the heart).

Note that 昔 has meaning of “past, ending”, hence connotations of “feelings for the past, feeling about an ending – regret, rue”.

慶.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
In {{w|oracle bone inscriptions}} {{Han compound|廌|心|t1=antelope|ls=ic}}. The component {{lang|zh|心}} signified goodness, blessings and auspiciousness. 

In {{w|bronze inscriptions}} {{lang|zh|心}} is often also written as {{lang|zh|文}}. During the Warring states period, the tail of {{zh-l|*廌}} corrupted into {{lang|zh|个}}, then further into {{lang|zh|夂}} during the Qin dynasty. Based on the overlap between the many ancient variants of the character {{zh-ref|Ji, Xusheng (2004)}} suggests that {{zh-l|*麕}}, {{zh-l|*廌}}, {{zh-l|*麐}}, {{zh-l|*鹿}}  all refer to kinds of deer – beautiful and therefore worth celebrating when obtained. He also concludes that there is enough evidence that {{lang|zh|慶}} and {{zh-l|*麐}} were once the same character. Since {{lang|zh|麐}} was the most precious among deer, its character was used to express celebration.

戴.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
In the {{w|oracle bone script}}, a {{liushu|p|nocap=y}} of a person holding something on their head with both hands. Possibly originally the same character as {{zh-l|*異}} (“person wearing a mask” or “person carrying something on their head”), see there for more.

Later, a {{liushu|psc|nocap=y}}, with {{och-l|異}} serving as the semantic (and also possibly phonetic) component, and either {{och-l|弋}}, {{och-l|𢦏}}, or {{och-l|之}} acting as a phonetic component.

房.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|戶|方|c1=s|c2=p|t1=door|ls=psc}}.

The meaning of [[方]] 'side' can also play a role, if the character originally referred to one of the two rooms beside the central room (side room).

才.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} — a sharp peg. Same as {{zh-l|*弋}} and {{zh-l|*尗}}. The sense "talent" is via an early phonetic loan. See also the top component of {{zh-l|*𢦏}}.

{{w|Xu Shen|Xu Shen}} erroneously analyzed this character as a sprout growing from the ground.

折.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
Originally {{zh-m|*㪿}}, an {{Han compound|𣏄|t1=a tree {{zh-m|*木}} cut in half|斤|t2=axe|alt1=⿱屮屮|ls=ic|nocap=y}} – an axe cutting a tree in half.

In the current form, the left side has become {{zh-m|*扌}} (“hand”).

授.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|手|t1=hand|受|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

There are actually three “hands” in this character, as {{zh-l|*爫}} also means “hand”, as does {{zh-l|*又}} graphically.

排.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|手|alt1=扌|t1=hand|非|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}} – to place in line.
{{wp|yue:,zh:}}

援.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|手|alt1=扌|爰|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=hand}}.

Possibly {{zh-l|*爰}} was the original form of this character, making it also an {{liushu|ic|nocap=y}}, see {{zh-l|*爰}} for more.

支.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|又|竹|alt2=十|t1=hand|t2=(half of {{zh-l|*竹}}) branch of bamboo|ls=ic}} – a branch.

Similar but unrelated to [[攴]], [[丈]], [[尹]] and [[父]].

教.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han etym|敎}}
{{Han compound|爻|子|攴|alt3=攵|t2=child|t3=teaching cane; stick that represents authority|c1=p|c2=s|c3=s|ls=psc}} – teaching a child. See also {{zh-l|*學}} and {{zh-l|*算}}.

敢.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|ic|nocap=y}}: a hand ({{zh-l|*又}}) using a capturing tool ({{zh-l|*𰅱}}) to hunt a wild boar ({{zh-l|*豕}}). Later, {{zh-l|*𰅱}} was omitted and a redundant component {{zh-l|*口}} or {{zh-l|*甘}} was added (Chen Jian, 2020; Xie Mingwen, 2020). 

The components eventually corrupted into the modern form.

散.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
The original version visible on oracle bone script is [[㪔]], hence {{Han compound|林|攴|ls=ic|t1=trees, forest|t2=to knock, to tap}}, also written with hemp stalks [[𣏟]] (> [[𢽳]]) or bamboo [[竹]] instead of two trees. The original meanings are "to clear a place from vegetation; to kill". The character later started meaning "to dissipate, to disperse" since bamboo in particular breaks easily into pieces; eventually, the meaning shifted to "to walk around, to stroll".

''{{w|Shuowen}}'': {{Han compound|肉|𢽳|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}, written as [[𣀙]], in which 林 is well visible on top.

文.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|i}}: a man with a painted or tattooed chest – original form of {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|紋}}. The tattoo was usually a cross, a V shape or a stylized heart. In current form, tattoo has vanished, so simply an outline of a man (head, outstretched arms, legs, chest) similar to [[立]]. Compare {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|大}}, which has no chest.

Unrelated to the Kangxi radicals [[齐]] and [[攵]].

方.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
Possibly the original character of {{och-l|旁|side}}, with its form related to {{och-l|巫}}. {{zh-l|巫}} depicts four extremities with indicator symbols, being the original character of {{och-l|荒}} in {{zh-l|四荒}}. Compared to {{och-l|巫}}, one end of this character lacks the indicator symbol and is elongated to denote "side" {{och-l|旁}}. {{och-l|方}} in {{zh-l|四方}} and {{och-l|荒}} in {{zh-l|四荒}} may be cognates.<ref>鄔可晶.據出土本説《老子》第四十一章“大方無隅”等句[J].道家文化研究,2025,():386-406.</ref>

It is unrelated to {{och-l|㫃|gloss=fluttering flag}}, from which characters like {{och-l|旗|gloss=flag}} and {{och-l|旅|gloss=troops}} are derived.

既.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|皀|t1=bowl of rice|旡|t2=kneeling figure|ls=ic}} – person kneeling at bowl of rice – sitting down to eat (rice already finished cooking), turning away (already finished eating) or abstaining from eating or consuming. 

Note the stroke order in the right component 旡, which may not be clear in the compressed form of this character: the left vertical and second horizontal are one stroke with a corner, while the upper right vertical continues in a diagonal stroke to lower left.

旨.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|ic}}: {{zh-l|*匕}} a spoon ({{zh-l|*匙}}) to eat or drink something delicious. The original version is [[𠤔]]. See [[嘗]]. Similar but unrelated to [[皀]].

According to the ''[[w:Shuowen Jiezi|Shuowen]]'': {{Han compound|匕|甘|t2=delicious|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|nocap=y}}.

旬.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}

{{Han compound|螾|日|t2=sun; day|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}.

The pictographic protoform of {{zh-l|*螾}} was borrowed to represent "period of days" in oracle bone script. In bronze inscriptions, the semantic component {{zh-l|*日|sun/day}} was added to this character. In the Warring States period, the phonetic component was mistaken as {{zh-l|*勹|bow down}}, hence the incorrect analysis in ''{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}'' of this character as {{Han compound|勹|日}}.

旺.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Simplified form of the original {{zh-l|*暀}}, a {{Han compound|日|往|t1=sun|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc|nocap=1}}.

The current form is reanalyzed as a {{Han compound|日|王|c1=s|c2=p|t1=sun|ls=psc|nocap=y}}.

易.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{w|Oracle bone script}} and {{w|bronze inscription}}s: a {{liushu|p|nocap=y}} of a filled container, the original form of {{och-l|賜|to confer, to bestow}}. Possibly specialised from {{och-l|益|to increase, to add}}. Perhaps borrowed phonetically to mean "easy".

The character is not the pictogram of a [[chameleon]], as folk etymology says. Unrelated to [[陽]] since it doesn't represent the [[sun]].

春.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|艸|屯|日|ls=psc|t1=grass|t2=swollen sprout|t3=sun|c1=s|c2=p|c3=s|tr=*tʰun|tr2=*dˤun;*trun;*dun}}. Some oracle-bone and bronze forms contain only two components, either {{och-l|*艸}}/{{och-l|*屮}} with {{och-l|*屯}}, or {{och-l|*日}} with {{och-l|*屯}}.

The form {{och-l|*萅}} preserves the older arrangement with {{och-l|*艸}}. In the regular script, the {{och-l|*艸}} and {{och-l|*屯}} have merged into {{och-l|*𡗗}}.

時.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=psc|日|c1=s|t1=sun; day|之|c2=p|t2=to go}} — the passing of time.

In ancient scripts, it had the form {{zh-l|*旹}}. The phonetic component later changed to {{och-l|*寺}}.

晴.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Written as {{zh-l|*夝}} in ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' and as {{zh-l|*星}} in the ''{{w|Classic of Poetry}}''.

{{Han compound|日|青|c1=s|c2=p|t1=sun|ls=psc}}.

曲.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|i}} – a bent object. Also {{Han simp|麴|nocap=y}} via the variant character {{l|zh|麯}}.

Unrelated to [[曹]] and [[典]].

曹.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

Originally {{zh-l|㯥}}, an {{liushu|ic|nocap=yes}}: two bundles {{zh-l|*東}}. The original meaning is "pair; peer, same kind". Later, {{zh-l|*囗}} was added as a distinguishing mark, which corrupted into {{zh-l|*甘}} in bronze inscriptions and {{zh-l|*曰}} in Seal Script, thus arriving at the intermediate character form {{zh-l|𣍘}}. The top two {{lang|zh|東}} have merged and now resemble {{m|zh|曲}} with an extra stroke.

Unrelated to {{lang|zh|曲}} and {{zh-l|*典}}.

月.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – pictographic representation of a [[crescent moon]]. Related to {{l|zh|夕}} and interchangeable in the past, but both are unrelated to [[多]], in which it represents two pieces of meat stylized as [[夕]].

Unrelated to:
* [[前]], in which it represents a boat [[舟]] ([[歬]] > [[𠝣]] > [[𣦃]] > 前);
* [[朕]], in which it represents a boat again ([[𦩎]] > [[𦨶]] > 朕)
* [[青]], in which it represents cinnabar [[丹]] ([[𤯞]] > [[靑]] > 青);
* [[贏]], in which it represents the elongated face of a kind of dragon, perhaps a [[蟠龍|Panlong]];
* [[祭]] and [[將]], in which it represents sacrificed meat stylized as [[𱼀]];
* [[然]], in which it represents dog meat ([[肰]]).
* [[有]], in which it represents meat.

服.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|凡|𠬝|ls=psc|t1=tray|t2=to subdue|c1=s|c2=p|tr2=*[b]ək}} – to serve. {{och-l|*𠬝}} is the original pictogram for this character; {{och-l|*凡}} was added later.

凡 here has been corrupted into {{zh-l|*舟}} and subsequently {{zh-l|*月}}.

朕.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han simp|𦩎|f=舟|t=月}}

Oracle-bone forms depict a {{zh-l|舟}} (“boat”) beside two hands holding a rod-like object. In {{zh-l|合集}} 20610, a variant shows one hand holding the object, which overlaps with the prow of the boat. The graph may be transcribed as {{zh-l|⿰舟尹}}, and has been identified with {{och-l|朕|tr=*lrəŋh}}. The graph may be interpreted as an ideograph for {{och-l|乘|tr=*mləŋ}} (“to mount; to ascend; to board”). <ref>鄔可晶,施瑞峰.説“朕”“灷”[J].文史,2022,(02):5-44.DOI:10.19325/j.cnki.11-1678/k.2022.02.014.</ref><ref>黄博：《甲骨文“朕*”、“䑣*”及相關字補釋》，《第二届“古文字與中華文明”國際學術論壇論文集》，2025年，第290頁</ref>

In Western Zhou bronze inscriptions, {{och-l|朕|tr=*lrəŋh}} is used as a first-person genitive pronoun of deference, commonly before terms for ancestors, parents, or rulers;<ref>朱其智,朱学斌.西周金文第一人称代词“余”“朕”和“我”的区别与混用[J].中国语文,2017,(02):171-181+255.</ref> it is also used as a loan for {{och-l|媵|tr=*ləŋh}} in the sense “to send; to escort; to accompany; to transfer”. 

The traditional sense “sign; trace; evidence”, also written {{och-l|眹|tr=*lrəŋh}}, may be related to {{och-l|證|tr=*təŋh}}, {{och-l|徵|tr=*trəŋ}} and {{och-l|應|tr=*ʔəŋ; ʔəŋh}}.

望.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally written {{zh-l|*𦣠}}, an {{Han compound|臣|𡈼|ls=ic|t1=eye|t2=person standing on the ground|nocap=y}} – a person standing up and looking off into the distance. Later {{zh-l|*月|moon}} was added to produce {{zh-l|*朢}}.

The {{zh-l|*臣}} component is now written {{zh-l|*亡}}, making the current form a {{Han compound|亡|月|𡈼|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|c3=s|t2=|t3=|nocap=y}}.

期.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|其|日|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}.

The original composition with the semantic {{och-l|*日|sun}} is preserved in the variant forms {{och-l|*㫷}} and {{och-l|*𣇳}}. The sun then changed to {{och-l|*月|moon}} to produce {{och-l|*朞}}, from which the character then evolved to the current form.

木.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – a tree: branches on top, roots on the bottom (more visible in earlier forms). 

See also {{zh-l|*本}}, {{zh-l|*未}}, and {{zh-l|*末}}.

朴.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|木|卜|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

Officially adopted as the simplified form of {{zh-l|*樸}} (etymology 6) in the ''Chinese Character Simplification Scheme'' ({{lw|zh|pref=《|s:漢字簡化方案|漢字簡化方案|suf=》}}) in 1956.

村.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|木|寸|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=tree}}.

Origin unclear. Seems a late graph, not in Shuowen dictionary of ca.100AD. Ogawa treats it as variant of CO graph {{zh-l|杶*}} "lacquer tree" which was used phonetically in the sense of "village" in place of another graph for "village", {{zh-l|邨*}}, which may be taken provisionally as {{zh-l|阝*}} "settlement, village", and {{zh-l|屯*}} "accumulate, stay" as semantic and phonetic.<ref>Henshall, Kenneth G; Seeley, Christopher（2016）The Complete Guide to Japanese Kanji {{ISBN|9784805311707}}</ref>

板.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|木|反|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=tree}}.

Officially adopted as the simplified form of {{zh-l|*闆}} (etymology 2) in the ''Chinese Character Simplification Scheme'' ({{lw|zh|pref=《|s:漢字簡化方案|漢字簡化方案|suf=》}}) in 1956.

枚.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Generally thought to be an {{Han compound|木|攴|ls=ic|t1=tree; wood|t2=hand holding a stick|nocap=1}}.

However, {{zh-ref|Zhengzhang (2003)}} considers it to be a {{Han compound|木|文|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=wood|nocap=1}}, where {{zh-l|*文}} was later corrupted to {{zh-l|*攴}}.

某.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|木|甘|t1=tree|t2=sweet|ls=ic}}; original form of {{och-l|梅|Chinese plum}}. Later borrowed to represent {{och-l|某|a certain}}.

Similar to [[果]] and [[栗]]. Similar but unrelated to [[柑]].

染.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|水|alt1=氵|t1=water|九|t2=hook|木|t3=tree|ls=ic}} – to hang (on a hook 九) from a tree (木) into water (氵), so as to dye.

Earlier forms more often graphically {{Han compound|水|杂|alt1=氵}}; in the modern form, the water radical is moved upward to the upper-left corner.

柵.originRaw:
{{Han etym|珊}}
{{Han etym|冊|no_img=y}}

{{Han compound|木|冊|ls=psc|ls2=ic|c1=s|c2=p|t1=tree; wood|t2=a form made by lining up pieces of wood and binding them together}}.<ref>Digital Shinjigen 2017</ref>

柿.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally written as {{l|zh|柹}}: {{Han compound|木|𠂔|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc}}.

The current form is {{Han compound|木|市|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc|nocap=1}}.

核.originRaw:
{{Han etym|核}}
{{Han etym|搰|no_img=y}}
{{Han compound|木|t1=tree|亥|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}} – a kind of tree. Modern meanings are borrowed.

棄.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=ic|子|𠀠|廾|t1=child|t2=basket|t3=two hands}} - throwing out a child in a basket. {{lang|zh|子}} was often inverted as {{lang|zh|𠫓}}.

In the oracle bone script form, {{lang|zh|子}} may appear with dots on either side of its head and body, perhaps representing amniotic fluid or dust.

款.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally {{m|zh|歀|tr=kuài}}, {{Han compound|ls=psc|柰|欠|c1=p|c2=s|t2=lacking, desire|nocap=yes|no_och=yes}}, meaning “desire”. The {{m|zh|木|tr=-}} component was simplified into {{m|zh|士|tr=-}} via {{w|libian}}, similar to {{m|zh|隸//|tr=-}}→{{m|zh|隷//|tr=-}}.<ref>李學勤，2012《字源》，天津：天津古籍出版社，2013年7月2次印刷。p. 768 </ref>

{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}} interprets it as {{Han compound|ls=psc|窾|欠|c1=p|c2=s|t2=lacking, desire|abb1=yes|nocap=yes}}.

正.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|丁|止|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|tr=*teŋ|tr1=*tˤeŋ}}

Oracle bone and Shang–Zhou bronze forms show {{m|zh|丁}} with {{m|zh|止}}. Some late Western Zhou and early Spring and Autumn bronze forms reduce {{m|zh|丁}} to a horizontal stroke joined to {{m|zh|止}}. Later bronze and Chu bamboo forms often show {{m|zh|上}} over {{m|zh|止}}; one-stroke forms also occur in Chu bamboo slips.

In oracle bone script, the graph is used for the word later written {{och-l|征|tr=*teŋ}}.

武.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|戈|止||t1=dagger-axe|t2=foot; to walk; to stop|ls=ic}} – army going on an expedition. In the character {{lang|zh|武}}, the {{lang|zh|戈}} component is written above the {{lang|zh|止}} component.

The graphical origin of {{lang|zh|武}} as “to stop violence” — the ultimate state of just warfare — is traditionally attributed to [[w:King Zhuang of Chu|King Zhuang of Chu]] {{defdate|597 {{BC}}}}:

{{zh-x|^楚 ^子 曰：「非 爾 所 知{zhī} 也。夫{fú} 文，止 戈 為{wéi} 武。」|Said Lord "Chǔ" (楚): “Not exactly as what you might have believed. The term itself means: stopping ("止") the blades ("戈") thus martiality ("武").”|CL|ref=Zuozhuan|collapsed=y}}

Linguistically, this was likely a misinterpretation, as {{zh-l|*止}} always means “to walk” ({{zh-l|*趾}}) when used as a radical, compare {{l|zh|步}}, {{l|zh|歷}} and {{l|zh|歧}}.

死.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|歹|尸|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}<ref>王鹏远.古汉字“变形音化”现象再研究[D].复旦大学,2022.DOI:10.27024/d.cnki.gfudu.2022.000001.
</ref>.

殿.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
''{{w|Shuowen}}'': {{Han compound|臀|alt1=𡱂|殳|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=hand holding a weapon}} – the sound of hitting (not attested in ancient documents).

{{zh-ref|Chi (2010)}} considers the character to be developed from {{zh-l|*𠂤|buttocks}}; {{zh-l|*尸|body}} and {{zh-l|*丌|pedestal}} are added to form {{zh-l|*𡱂|buttocks}}, and {{zh-l|*殳}} is added to emphasize the meaning of “hitting the buttocks”.

毀.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
* {{zh-ref|Sears}} interprets it as {{Han compound|ls=ic|臼|工|殳|t1=cracked skull|t2=tool|t3=hand action}}, a man being hit in the head.
* {{zh-ref|Liu Zhao}} interprets it as {{Han compound|ls=psc|𬛸|殳|c1=p|c2=s|t2=hand holding staff; strike}}
* Shuowen Jiezi interprets it as {{Han compound|ls=psc|毇|土|c1=p|c2=s|abb1=yes}}

毒.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Chen, Jian (2020): {{liushu|p}} of a woman wearing a feathered ornament on her head – original form of {{och-l|纛|feathered ornament}}. Later borrowed for {{och-l|毒|poison}}. If this is the correct interpretation, see also {{zh-l|*每}}.

Alternatively, Li Xueqin (2012) proposes it is a {{Han compound|屮|毐|c1=s|c2=p|t1=vegetation|ls=psc|nocap=y}}, meaning "a type of poisonous plant."

比.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
Chu Wan-li (1948): {{Han compound|ls=psc|匕|匕|c1=p|c2=p}}.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' interprets it as an ideogrammic compound meaning to juxtapose. Graphically similar but unrelated to {{och-l|*从}}, {{och-l|*尼}}, {{och-l|*化}} and {{och-l|*北}}.

氏.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}

{{liushu|p}}. Various interpretations exist: a man bending over to sow the field (see also [[氐]] > [[低]] and [[昏]]),<ref>[http://www.donga.com/news/article/all/20050424/8182795/1] Retrieved 2019-12-11</ref> the root of a tree (see also [[柢]]),<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20191210193027/https://hanja.dict.naver.com/hanja?q=%E6%B0%8F&cp_code=0&sound_id=0] Retrieved 2019-12-11</ref> a man bowing over to the right, a stick with a decoration on top to represent a clan, a spoon (according to Guo Moruo and due to similarity to [[匕]]), a mallet, a man holding an object, etc.

On bronze inscriptions, a black dot was added inside the vertical stroke of the character; then, this point turned into a horizontal stroke 一 while the vertical stroke mutated into a curved trait.

水.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} of water flowing; sometimes written as the variant [[氺]] (e.g., [[泰]], [[录]], and [[黎]]). Next to a character, it is compressed and stylized into [[氵]] (which is unrelated to [[冫]]).

Compare {{och-l|川}} and its variant [[巛]]. Compare also [[永]] and its cognate [[𠂢]]. See also the bottom part of [[原]] in its original version ([[厡]]).

永.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} of a long river with [[tributary|tributaries]]. Cognate with {{zh-l|*𠂢}}, {{zh-l|*派}}. 

Alternatively, {{liushu|p|nocap=y}} of a person swimming in the water. Original form of {{och-l|泳}}.

汽.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|水|气|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|alt1=氵|t1=water}}.

Alternatively, an {{Han compound|水|气|alt1=氵|t1=water|t2=air|ls=ic}} - steam. Quite possibly both of these origins could be true, out of convenience.

法.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
[[File:晚商作册般青铜鼋.jpg|thumb|X-ray image of the ''Zuoceban yuan'' inscription, Late Shang dynasty]]
Earliest attested on the late Shang bronze {{lw|zh|作册般銅黿}}. {{Han compound|水|alt1=氵|廌|盍|t1=water|t2=a type of antelope or caprid|c1=s|c2=s|c3=p|abb3=y|ls=psc|nocap=y|tr=*pap|tr3=*gˤap}}. Later, {{zh-l|廌}} was omitted.

In bronze inscriptions, mostly used for {{och-l|廢|to cast aside; to waste|tr=*pas}}:
{{zh-x|率 亡 '''@灋'''{fèi} 矢。|not a single arrow was '''wasted'''.|CL-PC|ref=Late {{w|Shang dynasty|Shang}}, {{lw|zh|作册般銅黿}} inscription|collapsed=y}}

A notable exception is the early Western Zhou {{lw|zh|大盂鼎}}, where {{zh-l|灋}} is analyzed as {{zh-l|*廢}} in the sense of "great" (per {{w|Erya}} {{lang|zh|《釋詁》：「廢，[[大]]也。」}}):
{{zh-x|…故 天 異 臨 子、'''@灋''' 保 先王、☐ 有 四方。|...therefore Heaven watched over you with extraordinary [favour], '''greatly''' safeguarded the former kings, and [they] possessed the four quarters.|CL-PC|ref=Early {{w|Western Zhou}}, {{w|Da Yu ding}} inscription|collapsed=y}}

津.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|水|c1=s|t1=water|盡|abb2=y|c2=p|ls=psc}}.

The phonetic component {{och-l|*𦘔}} is, according to Ji Xusheng (2014), a simplified form of {{och-l|*盡}}. It further corrupted into {{och-l|*聿}} in the clerical script.

火.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – pictographic representation of a fire. Written as [[灬]] when at the bottom of a character, but still seen in the original form in the character [[灸]].

See also the top component of [[叟]] in its original form and the bottom part of [[赤]].

災.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|𡿧|火|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t1=flood calamity|t2=fire|tr=*tsˤə;tsˤəh}}.

From {{zh-l|𡿧}}, an oracle-bone graph of three zigzag strokes depicting flowing water; forms with a horizontal stroke phoneticize the middle stroke as {{och-l|才|tr=*dzˤə}}. <ref>陳劍「說「昔」字並論「𡿧」形的真正來源」『饒宗頤國學院院刊』第八期 香港浸會大學饒宗頤國學院、2021年、1–46頁。</ref>

為.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han etyl|爲}}
{{Han simp|爲}}.

In the oracle bone script, it is an {{Han compound|又|t1=hand|象|t2=elephant|ls=ic|nocap=1}} — a hand holding an elephant. The {{zh-l|*又}} later became {{zh-l|*爪|hand; claw}}.

''{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}'' interprets the character as a representation of the female macaque.

無.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}}. A person dancing with [[oxtail|ox tails]] held in both hands to pray for rain.

This character is borrowed for “have no”, and the character {{l|zh|舞}} is used for the original sense.

然.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|肰|火|t2=fire|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}} – to burn. The derivative {{och-l|燃}} preserves the original word.

In the modern form, the top-left component {{och-l|*肉|meat}} has been turned on its side, appearing more like {{zh-l|*夕|evening}} with an extra stroke ({{l|mul|sc=Hani|𱼀}}); compare {{zh-l|*祭}}, {{zh-l|*將}}.

熊.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
According to ''{{w|Shuowen}}'', {{Han compound|能|炎|c1=s|c2=p|t1=bear|ls=psc|abb2=y|nocap=1}}.

This character originally represented an onomatopoetic word ({{zh-l|熊熊|''(of flame)'' raging}}). Later its semantic component {{och-l|能}}, the character for the Old Chinese word "bear", was borrowed for another word. This character thus began to represent the word "bear" instead.

片.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Mirror image of {{och-l|爿|id=1|piece}}, which is the pictogram of a bed.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'': half of {{zh-l|*木|wood; tree}} to denote “to pare; to slice”; but this interpretation is not based on oracle bones and bronzes.

理.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|玉|里|c1=s|c2=p|t1=jade|ls=psc}}.

According to ''{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}'', the character meant “to polish jade”. The current meaning “to order, tidy up” may be derived from it.

瓶.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|并|瓦|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=earthenware}}. According to the radical, it originally indicates an earthenware vessel, i.e., not a glass bottle. It had a long slender neck opening up to a narrow mouth. However, 瓶 was also part of ritual bronzes in ancient China, hence it was made of bronze when used during rituals.

Today, it indicates bottles of any kind and dimension.

田.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – pictographic representation of a field divided into four sections. See the original version of [[甫]] and [[里]].
Sometimes it means mask. See [[異]] and [[鬼]]. Unrelated to [[思]], [[黄]], [[果]], [[由]], [[甲]], and perhaps [[番]].

由.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – pictogram of new shoots or leaves growing from {{zh-l|木}}; possibly the original graph of {{zh-l|㽕}}, with its upper part later extracted as {{zh-l|由}}.<ref>[1]田秋棉,陈絜.商周⿰㽕尋、寻、谭诸地的纠葛及相关历史问题之检讨[J].史学集刊,2021,(01):84-91.DOI:10.19832/j.cnki.0559-8095.2021.0007.</ref><ref>陳聞達：《釋甲骨文中的「㽕」、「𣡌」》</ref>

Alternatively interpreted as the upper part of the oracle-bone form of {{zh-l|柚}} (“pomelo”), depicting the fruit itself.<ref>王子楊《釋甲骨文中的“柚”》，《甲骨文字形類組差異現象研究》，中西書局，2013年，第287—307頁。</ref><ref>鄔可晶,施瑞峰.説“朕”“灷”[J].文史,2022,(02):5-44.DOI:</ref>

畜.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|玄|田|ls=p|t1=rope tying the bag of feed|t2=bag of feed}} – a bag of feed, hence livestock.

Alternatively, {{Han compound|玄|[[田]] or [[囿]]|t1=rope|t2=pen; fold; enclosure}} – tying animals and raising them in the enclosure.

疾.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=psc|疒|疾|alt2=⿰大矢|c1=p|c2=p|t1=illness|t2=fast}}. The two characters, originally representing different words, have merged into one character representing both senses.

The oracle glyph of {{lang|zh|sc=Hani|⿰大矢}} depicts an arrow flying towards a person - "fast". The "illness" sense preserves the original meaning of {{och-l|*疒}}. The {{och-l|*亠}} component represents the person found in both pictograms.

的.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|白|勺|c1=s|c2=p|t1=white|ls=psc}}.

The original form was {{zh-l|*旳}} with the meaning of “bright”, hence the initial {{zh-l|*日}} semantic. See Etymology 1 below.

皮.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – a hand ({{zh-l|*又}}) stripping the fur from an animal pelt (similar to {{zh-l|*革}}). The pelt moved to the top left and became abstracted.

The character [[叚]] is similar, but actually it depicts two hands and some rocks 石 on oracle bones; a conservative variant is [[𠭊]].

益.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|水|t1=water|皿|t2=vessel|ls=ic}}. Original form of {{och-l|溢|to overflow}}. Upper component resembles {{och-l|氺|water}} in a horizontal form.

Compare {{zh-l|*易}}.

盟.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{w|Oracle bone script}} ({{zh-l|*𥁰}}): {{Han compound|囧|皿|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

{{w|Bronze inscriptions}} ({{zh-l|*𥂗}}): {{Han compound|朙|皿|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}. {{zh-l|*月}} was added to {{zh-l|*𥁰}}. {{zh-l|*朙}} is an ancient form of {{zh-l|*明}}, and now this character ({{lang|zh|盟}}) is a {{Han compound|明|皿|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|nocap=y}}.

監.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|臥|皿|ls=ic|t1=a person with an emphasized eye|t2=container}}: a person looking into a container ({{zh-ref|Ji Xusheng, 2004}}). Original form of {{och-l|鑑|mirror}}. 
The container later corrupted into {{och-l|*血}}; in the regular script, this additional stroke has migrated to below the {{och-l|*人}} → {{lang|zh|sc=Hani|⿱𠂉丶}}/{{lang|zh|sc=Hani|⿱𠂉一}}.

盤.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|般|皿|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref>.

{{och-l|*凡}} originally referred to this word, {{och-l|*殳}} and {{och-l|*皿}} were added later. The {{och-l|*凡}} has also corrupted into {{zh-l|*舟}}.

石.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}}: a stone beneath a cliff ({{zh-l|*厂}}). The cliff was subsequently distorted into {{zh-l|*丆}}; compare [[叚 ]], [[段]] as well as [[度]], [[席]].

Alternatively, a cave set into the side of a cliff or mountain.

神.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|示|申|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=[[god]]; [[deity]]|t2=[[lightning]]}}.

Originally {{zh-l|*申}} in the oracle bone script and in some bronze inscriptions. The ancients feared the [[unpredictable]] [[lightning]] and considered it to be [[divine]] or of divine [[origin]]. {{zh-l|*示}} was added for specialization.

票.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{Han simp|𤐫|f=火|t=示|f2=𢍱|t2=覀}}. {{Han compound|火|𢍱|ls=ic|c1=s|c2=s|t1=fire|t2=to rise high}}, which represents two pairs of hands holding and rising an object, perhaps a bag or basket ([[西]]). The character originally represented the idea of sparks rising up. By extension, it was also used to mean "token" or "label."<ref>Digital Shinjigen 2017</ref> Then, the two pair of hands disappeared in the modern version.

Unrelated to the original version of [[要]] and [[栗]].

秘.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
In current form: {{Han compound|禾|必|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

Variant ({{zh-l|*俗字}}) of {{och-l|祕}} via {{zh-l|*示}} → {{zh-l|*禾}}, hence there is a reference to secret rituals and the concept of arcane.

租.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|禾|且|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc}}.

Land rent was paid in grain.

稚.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|禾|t1=rice|屖|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

During the Han Dynasty, the {{zh-l|屖}} component was commonly substituted with {{zh-l|隹}}.

稽.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
Originally {{zh-l|*𩒨}}, a {{Han compound|旨|頁|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=person's head|nocap=y}}.

In the modern form it is a {{Han compound|𥝌|尤|旨|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=s|c3=p|t1=bent, stunted tree|t2=|nocap=y}}.

竹.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – two bamboo stalks, with leaves. Earlier forms resembled [[个]] + [[个]], current form resembles rather [[ケ]] + [[ケ]], or [[亇]] + [[亇]].

See also the original version of [[散]].

笑.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally {{Han compound|艸|犬|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t1=grass|t2=dog|nocap=y}}.
Later, {{Han compound|竹|夭|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|nocap=y}}.

第.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|竹|alt1=𥫗|弟|c1=s|c2=p|t1=bamboo|ls=psc}}. {{zh-l|*竹}} here refers to the bamboo strips used for writing (e.g. accounting, classification) in ancient times. See also {{zh-l|*等}}.

Alternatively, may have originally been written {{zh-l|*弟}}.

等.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|竹|t1=bamboo|寺|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

{{zh-l|*竹}} here refers to the bamboo strips used for writing (e.g. grading, classification) in ancient times. {{och-l|寺}} was originally used phonetically; the character was subsequently borrowed for another word with the same meaning, but with no phonetic resemblance to {{zh-l|*寺}}.

米.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – rice kernels without husk. The character [[粟]], by contrast, refers to rice kernels with husk. According to Li Xiaoding (李孝定), the horizontal stroke is a bamboo sieve ([[籭]] or [[篩子]]). See also the bottom component of [[暴]] in its original form.

Unrelated to [[木]], [[禾]], and [[来]].

粧.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{Han compound|米|庄|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=rice}}. It conveys the meaning of "face powder" and, by extension, is used to mean "to adorn oneself."
Originally, it was a popular variant of the character [[妝]].<ref>Digital Shinjigen 2017</ref>

素.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – hands {{zh-l|*廾}} braiding thread {{zh-l|*糸}}.

In modern form, top component looks like {{zh-l|*生}} without the diagonal stroke – compare {{zh-l|*責}}, {{zh-l|*青}}.

索.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=ic|糸|𣎳|t1=thread|t2=strip fibres off a hemp stalk}}. Threads (rope) being removed from a fabric.

{{zh-l|*糸}} visually represents the middle interior of the small seal script form, whereas {{zh-l|*𣎳}} is the three-pointed top part and the left and right lines.

累.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han simp|纍|f=畾|t=田}}.

Folk etymology considers this character as an ideographic compound representing the [[tiredness]] after work. In ancient China, men were working in the field ({{zh-l|*田}}) while women sewed ({{zh-l|*糸}}).

缶.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – an earthenware jar with a lid. See also the bottom part of the original versions of [[由]].

Unrelated to the top component of [[鬱]], in which it represents a stylized man ([[大]]).

羨.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|羊|㳄|ls=ic|t1=sheep|t2=saliva}} – to envy.

{{zh-l|*羊}} may be interpreted as a beautiful sheep or good mutton (cf. {{zh-l|*美}} “beautiful” and {{zh-l|*善}} “good”).

The ''Kangxi Dictionary'', following {{lang|zh|《佩觿》}}, enters the word in etymology 2 as {{zh-l|*羡}}, separating it from {{zh-l|*羨}} (etymology 1). This is also followed by dictionaries such as ''{{w|Hanyu Da Cidian}}'' and ''{{w|Dai Kan-Wa Jiten}}'', but ''{{w|Zhongwen Da Cidian}}'' disagrees with this treatment. (''{{w|Hanyu Da Zidian}}'' regards {{zh-l|*羡}} as the main form of all pronunciations.) According to {{lang|zh|《說文解字羣經正字》}}:
{{zh-x|羨，貪欲 也，今 經典 往往 作 羡。@^郭.^忠恕《@^佩觿》分 羨、羡 爲 二 字。羡，以 脂 翻，江夏 地名；羨，似 面 翻，慕 也。非 也，字書 無 从 次 之 字，惟 一 字 而 數 音 耳。|{{lang|zh|羨}}, meaning "to covet", is now frequently written as {{lang|zh|羡}} in the classics. {{w|Guo Zhongshu}}'s ''Peixi'' distinguishes {{lang|zh|羨}} and {{lang|zh|羡}} as separate characters. {{lang|zh|羡}}, fanqie {{lang|zh|以脂}}, name of a place in Jiangxia. {{lang|zh|羨}}, fanqie {{lang|zh|似面}}, to desire. This is not the case. There's no such character with the {{lang|zh|次}} element in character dictionaries. There is only one character but several pronunciations.|CL|tr={{lang|zh|羨}} (Xiàn), tānyù yě, jīn jīngdiǎn wǎngwǎng zuò {{lang|zh|羡}}. Guō Zhōngshù “Pèixī” fēn {{lang|zh|羨}} (xiàn), {{lang|zh|羡}} (yí) wéi èr zì. Yí, yǐ zhī fān, Jiāngxià dìmíng; xiàn, sì miàn fān, mù yě. Fēi yě, zìshū wú cóng {{lang|zh|次}} (cì) zhī zì, wéi yī zì ér shù yīn ěr.|ref={{lang|zh|《說文解字羣經正字》}}, quoted from ''{{w|Zhongwen Da Cidian}}''|collapsed=y}}

老.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|人|t1=man|毛|t2=hair|匕|t3=cane|ls=ic}} – a man with long hair (an old man), leaning on a cane. Compare top component to {{och-l|孝}}.

Cognate to {{och-l|考}}; the most commonly cited example of {{zh-l|*轉注|reciprocal meaning}}.

考.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
Variant of {{och-l|老}} – {{Han compound|耂|t1=an old man with long hair, bent over|丂|t2=a walking cane|ls=ic}} originally drawn as [[匕]] – an old man, leaning on a cane. Compare top component to {{och-l|孝}}.

Structurally related to {{och-l|老}}; the most commonly cited example of {{zh-l|*轉注|reciprocal meaning}}.

聞.originRaw:
{{etystub|zh|ancient glyph form images}}
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|門|耳|c1=p|c2=s|t2=ear|ls=psc}}. Compare {{och-l|問}}.

肖.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|小|月|c1=p|c2=s|t2=moon|ls=psc}}. The character may have been the original form of {{och-l|宵|night}}.

''{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}'' has erroneously analyzed the character as a {{Han compound|小|肉|c1=p|c2=s|t2=meat|ls=psc|nocap=1}}.

股.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally written {{och-l|夃|thigh}}, according to {{lang|zh|趙平安}}. Later the  {{lang|zh|夃}} component came to be written {{m|zh|殳|tr=-}}, and a {{m|zh|肉|tr=-}} component was added, making the modern form an {{Han compound|肉|夃|t1=body|t2=thigh|c1=s|c2=p|ls=ic|ls2=psc|nocap=y}}.

Shuowen incorrectly considered it {{Han compound|肉|t1=body|殳|t2=action|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc}} – the hips as an active part of the body.

肥.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}: {{Han compound|ls=ic|肉|卪|t1=meat|t2=person kneeling}}.
{{zh-ref|Ji Xusheng (2004)}} posits that its actually a {{Han compound|ls=psc|肉|妃|c1=s|c2=p|abb2=yes|nocap=yes}}.

育.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Evolved geometrically from {{l|och|胄}} in Han dynasty era ({{zh-ref|Liu, 2023}}).

Before that, Pronunciation 1 was written as {{l|mul|毓}}; see there for more.

膝.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|肉|alt1=⺼|t1=meat, body|桼|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}} – the knee.

Originally {{zh-l|*厀}}, a {{Han compound|桼|卩|c1=p|c2=s|t2=kneeling person|ls=psc|nocap=y}}.

臣.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – A vertical eye of a man looking downwards. As an individual character it has taken on the meanings “slave; servant; minister”, while the meaning relating to eyes appears when used as a radical, such as in {{zh-l|*臨|to look down at}} and {{zh-l|*監|to inspect}}.

Compare {{zh-l|*目}}. Unrelated to [[𦣞]].

臨.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|臣|人|品|c1=s|c2=s|c3=p|t1=eye|t2=person|t3=objects|ls=ic|ls2=psc}} – a person looking down at some objects.

Alternatively analyzed as a {{Han compound|臥|品|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc|nocap=yes}}.

自.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}}: a nose — original form of {{och-l|鼻|nose}}. Later phonetically borrowed as an instance of {{liushu|j|nocap=y}} for {{och-l|自|oneself; from}}.

Folk etymology considers the meaning to arise from a habit of pointing at one’s nose to indicate oneself.

至.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|i}}: an arrow stuck into the ground. Some folk etymologies interpret the whole pictogram as an eagle catching a prey.

Compare [[畀]] and [[矢]]. See also the bottom component of [[睪]], the original versions of [[弗]] and the top component of [[𢦏]].

興.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|ic}} – four hands ({{zh-l|*舁}}) lifting an object. 

In oracle bone script and early bronze inscriptions, the central object had various shapes (such as a lifting tray, sometimes resembling {{zh-l|*井}}). It eventually standardized into {{zh-l|*同}} in later scripts.

舟.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – a boat.

Later sometimes written as {{zh-l|*月}}, {{zh-l|*肉}} or {{zh-l|*凡}} in components.

若.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – a person combing their hair; [[obedient]].

Later {{zh-l|*口}} was added and the original component came to be written {{zh-l|*叒}}.

茂.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|艸|戊|c1=s|c2=p|t1=grass|ls=psc}}.

Applying this character to mean “[[cyclopentadiene]]” in organic chemistry results from the decomposition of the character as an {{Han compound|艸|戊|t1=radical for [[aromatic]] compounds|t2=five-carbon ring|ls=ic|nocap=1}}, since the [[conjugate base]] of cyclopentadiene (cyclopentadienyl anion) is aromatic.

茶.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|艸|alt1=艹|余|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc}}.

{{lang|zh|茶}}, then slightly modified into a more conservative 茶, originates as a graphical modification of archaic {{m|och|荼||bitter plant}}, used for “tea” in classical sources. The modern version is unrelated to the bottom right component of {{lang|zh|[[條]] 
}}.

荒.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|艸|alt1=艹|t1=grass, plant|巟|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.
Note: The middle part could be found as [[亾]] instead of [[亡]].

華.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}}; this was the original form of {{och-l|花|flower}}. In the bronze inscription form, the top part of the glyph resembles flowers of a plant, and the bottom part ({{zh-l|*于}}) resembles its stalk and leaves. Also {{liushu|psc|nocap=1|adj=1}}, with {{och-l|于}} functioning as the phonetic part. In the seal script, {{zh-l|*艸|grass; plant}} was added as a semantic component.

{{lang|zh|華}} ({{zh-l|*𦻏}}) and {{zh-l|*𠌶}} were initially the same character, but they were erroneously split in ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' into two entries.

蓋.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|艸|盍|c1=s|t1=grass|c2=p|ls=psc}} — a thatch.

{{l-lite|zh|sc=Hani|盍}} is the original character for the “cover” sense. However, because this original character was borrowed to mean “why not”, the scope of senses for {{lang-lite|zh|sc=Hani|蓋}} expanded to include the “cover” sense (and its extended meanings).

蔑.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|𦰋|戈|ls=ic|t1=person|t2=dagger-axe}} – a weapon striking a person's lower leg — to obliterate.

{{zh-l|*𦰋}} (“person”) was composed of an eye with eyebrows (now written {{zh-l|*眉}}) on top of {{zh-l|*人}}. The eye and eyebrows are written {{lang|zh|sc=Hani|⿱⻀罒}} in the modern form, and {{zh-l|*人}} has combined with {{zh-l|*戈}} to form {{zh-l|*戍}}.

虹.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally {{liushu|p|adj=1|nocap=1}} in the oracle bone script. The ancient Chinese regarded the rainbow as a two-headed dragon, as in an oracle-bone divination inscription:
{{zh-x|有 出 虹 自 北 飲 于 河。|A rainbow came forth from the north and drank from the River.|ref=oracle-bone inscription|CL|collapsed=y}}

In the seal script, it has become a {{Han compound|虫|工|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=insect; creature|nocap=1}}.

血.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|一|皿|t1=drop of blood|t2=container|ls=ic}} - a drop of blood inside a [[chalice]] for sacrificial uses. In the oracle bone script, the character was used to refer to killing animals to obtain blood to sacrifice to ancestors:<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref>
{{zh-x|血 三 羊。|to '''(ritually) bleed''' three sheep|CL-PC|collapsed=y|ref=''{{w|Jiaguwen Heji}}'' #22231}}
{{zh-x|在\才{zài}  血 室|to be in the '''blood (sacrifice)''' hall|CL-PC|collapsed=y|ref=''{{w|Jiaguwen Heji}}'' #41091}}

行.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – a street intersection. Original form of {{och-l|行|id=1|place}}. Later {{Han phonetically borrowed}} to represent {{och-l|行|id=2|to go}}.

Originally symmetric, it has been simplified asymmetrically; the left half {{l|zh|彳}} is widely used as a radical, while the right half {{l|zh|亍}} finds occasional use, and the character can be broken up as {{Han compound|彳|亍}}, though originally it was not a compound.

術.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|行|朮|t1=street, path|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc}}.

For the “to throw” definition, from {{bor|zh|en|shoot}}.

街.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|行|圭|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc}}.

Originally, the phonetic component is not related to the concept of earth ([[土]]) since in its original form it represents a pair of ritual axes perhaps made of jade [[士]]. Hence, the link to the concept of earth in this character and [[厓]] either comes from folk etymology or its appearance in the Small Seal script.

衣.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} Outline of the chest, upper clothing. Compare to the upper part of {{zh-l|*文}}.

This pictogram 衣 can be split into two parts and hold another component (e.g., [[裹]], [[衮]], [[褱]], [[襄]], and [[𧝑]] as used in [[㞡]] > [[展]]). See also the top component of [[雜]].

表.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally {{zh-l|*𧘝}}, an {{Han compound|衣|毛|ls=ic|t1=garment; clothing|t2=hair; feather|nocap=1}} — outer garment.

Officially adopted as the simplified form of {{zh-l|*錶}} (etymology 2) in the ''Chinese Character Simplification Scheme'' ({{lw|zh|pref=《|s:漢字簡化方案|漢字簡化方案|suf=》}}) in 1956.

裂.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|列|衣|t2=clothes|ls=psc|ls2=ic|c1=p|c2=s|t1=to tear}} — to cut and tear clothes.
The phonetic {{m|zh|列|tr=-}} depicts bones being cut up with a blade and arranged in a line.

襲.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|c1=p|c2=s|ls=psc|龖|衣|t2=cloth}}.

The bronze inscription and ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' ''zhouwen'' forms were in the form of {{zh-l|*𧟟}}. The ''Shuowen'' small seal script form and subsequent forms are in the form of {{lang|zh|襲}}, as the phonetic component {{och-l|*龖}} has been reduced to {{zh-l|*龍}}.

西.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

The oracle-bone forms of {{och-l|西|tr=*sˤən}} fall into two types: open-topped and closed-topped. The open-topped type is found only in periods I–II, especially in the Bin and Chu groups; later scripts inherit the closed-topped type.

The open-topped form has been interpreted as a bird's nest, possibly related to {{och-l|栖|tr=*sˤəj}} (“to perch; to roost”). The later closed-topped form may be related to {{och-l|囟|tr=*sənh}}: in the Zhouyuan oracle-bone inscriptions, {{zh-l|囟}} is used for {{zh-l|西}}.

要.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}}: a man {{zh-l|*大}} then drawn as a woman ({{zh-l|*女}}) with two hands pointing to his/her midsection. On top of the character there was huge eye 目, then stylized as a horn 角. The hands gradually shifted upwards; hence, a nearly original version of the character is [[𡢗]]. All the upper part was eventually stylized as {{zh-l|*覀}}. Unrelated to [[栗]] but partly related to [[票]].

Original meaning was “waist” (now {{och-l|腰}}).

視.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|示|見|c1=p|c2=s|t2=see|ls=psc}}.

Related to {{zh-l|*見|to see; to meet}}. The Chu form did not include the phonetic {{zh-l|*示}} component, making it near-identical to {{zh-l|*見}}, but with the lower component a kneeling figure rather than a standing one. ({{zh-ref|Galambos, 2006}})

親.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Bronze script: {{Han compound|辛|見|t2=see|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|nocap=1}}.

Seal script: {{Han compound|𣓀|見|t2=see|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|nocap=1}}. The left part (a chisel with a tree at the bottom to indicate "hazelnut branch" used as a whip) was then stylized as 亲, which originally is a variant of 辛, the chisel.

討.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=psc|肘|言|c1=p|c2=s|abb1=yes}}.

''{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}'' interprets it as an {{Han compound|ls=ic|言|寸|t1=speech|t2=hand|nocap=yes}} – rectify.

詣.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
By ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' and ''{{w|ja:字統#字通|Jitsū}}'', and following Zhengzhang (2003), a {{Han compound|ls=psc|言|旨|c1=s|c2=p|nocap=1}}.

Alternatively, {{Han compound|ls=ic|言|旨|t1=speech|t2=delicious|nocap=1}} — word use so skilled as to be delicious. The first interpretation is seemingly most accepted.

語.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|言|吾|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|tr=*ŋaʔ; *ŋah|tr2=*ŋa; *ŋˤa}}.

Part of a *''ŋa''- word family centred on "to face; to respond to": {{och-l|御|to drive; to resist; to welcome; to attend upon|tr=*ŋˤrah}}, {{och-l|禦|to defend; to resist|tr=*ŋaʔ}}, {{och-l|圉|to guard; border|tr=*ŋaʔ}}, {{och-l|迓|to welcome; to meet in battle|tr=*ŋˤrah}}, {{och-l|逆|to meet; to oppose|tr=*ŋrak}}, {{och-l|晤|to meet face to face|tr=*ŋˤah}}, {{och-l|忤|to go against|tr=*ŋˤah}}. The original sense was likely "to face someone with speech; speech directed at an interlocutor", as opposed to {{och-l|言|tr=*ŋan}}, which denotes the speech act itself. Compare {{zh-ref|Shuowen}}: {{lang|zh|直言曰言，論難曰語}}.

豆.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
[[File:Illustration of Dou 豆.svg|thumb|class=skin-invert-image|Illustration of Dou 豆]]
{{liushu|p}} – some kind of container. Borrowed phonetically for the plant name. Displaced Old Chinese {{zh-l|菽}}. Unrelated to the bottom part of [[豊]].

貌.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|豹|皃|c1=p|abb1=y|c2=s|t2=looks|ls=psc}}.

Originally written as {{och-l|皃}}, which is a {{liushu|p|nocap=1}} of the face.

貫.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}}: two {{och-l|*貝|shell}} strung together.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'' mistakes the character for a compound of {{och-l|*毌}} + {{och-l|*貝}}.

責.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|朿|貝|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=[[cowry]]; money}} – tax; debt.

Top component now resembles {{zh-l|*生}} (without the diagonal stroke) – compare {{zh-l|*青}}, {{zh-l|*素}}.

買.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|网|貝|t1=net|t2=money cowrie|ls=ic}}. The original version is [[𧵽]].

{{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart (2014)}} consider it to be a {{Han compound|羅|貝|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=money cowrie|no_och=1|tr1=*rˤaj|abb1=1|nocap=1}}.

賊.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|則|戈|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=weapon}}.

This was later reinterpreted as a compound of {{zh-l|*貝|money}} and {{zh-l|*戎|weapon}} to produce the modern form.

賓.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|𡧍|貝|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}.<ref>謝明文.釋甲骨文中的“𫤫”及相關諸字——兼論丏、亥係一形分化[J].出土文献与古文字研究,2021,(00):42-67.
</ref>

赤.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|大|t1=person, big|火|t2=fire|ls=ic}} – the color of fire (a person by a fire). The original version is [[𤆍]].

The man on top is simplified as 土 as in [[去]] and [[走]] (< [[𧺆]]). The fire is stylized as well, but an intermediate and obsolete variant is [[灻]].

軍.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|psc}}: semantic {{zh-l|*車|chariot}} + phonetic {{zh-l|*勹}}. The phonetic component here is not {{och-l|勹}}, but the original form of {{och-l|螾}}, which also acts as the phonetic component of {{och-l|旬}}, {{och-l|勻}} and {{och-l|云}}. 

The {{w|Shuowen Jiezi}} interprets it as {{Han compound|車|勹|ls=ic|t1=cart; chariot|t2=to surround|nocap=yes}}.

軟.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally written {{zh-l|*輭}}, a {{Han compound|ls=psc|車|耎|c1=s|c2=p|t1=carriage|nocap=y}}. {{lang|zh|耎}} turned into {{lang|zh|欠}} over time due to a popular variant.

The meaning of the carriage is not fully clear. It could be linked to the synonym {{zh-l|*柔}}, which indicates the wood soft enough to be cut, or it could indicate the concept of being tired and weak after dragging a heavy carriage, hence "soft"; the right component could add part of the meaning, since it originally depicts a man under the rain, hence getting soaked (perhaps while dragging a heavy carriage). The modern component 欠 depicts a man kneeling with an open mouth, perhaps while breathing due to exhaustion.

較.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|車|交|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=vehicle}}.

The original phonetic component was {{och-l|爻}}, later corrupted to {{och-l|交}}

辛.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} Older oracle bone forms of 辛 (⿱∇木) depict newly grown tree shoots, acting as the original ideogram for {{och-l|*新|tr=*siŋ}} and {{och-l|*薪|tr=*siŋ}}. In {{zh-l|*新}}, the top of the axe component ({{zh-l|*斤}}) aligns with the bottom of ∇, signifying the chopping of wood.<ref>張昂「説“辛”“新”“薪”」『語言科学』第24巻第3期（総第136期）2025年、322-336頁。（[https://oversea.cnki.net/kcms2/article/abstract?v=nTS8fe6ew4oGJd99kw7YBDZGcSHiT2aTwp21JYw_isvYMm76Ahp748E27LBvLYctlOmeWu_Aw3ysGTFwhljVtgR1LdgYkWUGPyPc45oKlYHqx2tViALQ02haSHU1ObRrhcDYsInczWjN_5T2CMAB9TUfp5At6WzEPKTrOJHQWUM=&uniplatform=OVERSEA CNKI]）</ref>

Traditionally interpreted as a pictogram of a tool—a chisel, awl, or spike—used to mark slaves and criminals. However, some scholars suggest this view stems from a conflation with the character {{zh-l|*䇂}}.

逆.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|辵|alt1=辶|t1=movement|屰|t2=go against|ls=ic|ls2=psc|c1=s|c2=p}} – going in the opposite direction, contrary, disobedient.

Also originally written {{zh-l|*屰}}, see there for more.

透.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|辵|秀|c1=s|c2=p|t1=walking|ls=psc}}.

This character is not attested in the {{w|oracle bone script}} or {{w|bronze inscriptions}}.

連.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|辵|alt1=辶|車|ls=ic|t1=to walk|t2=chariot}} – man-drawn carriage.

According to {{w|Duan Yucai}}, {{m|zh|聯}} and {{lang|zh|連}} are two characters for a same word, with the former used in the {{w|Zhou dynasty}} and the latter in the {{w|Han dynasty}}.

進.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|辵|隹|t1=walk|t2=short-tail bird|ls=ic}}. A bird can only walk forward, not backward, hence implying "forward". ''{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}'' interprets it as
{{Han compound|辵|閵|ls=psc|c1=s|abb2=2|c2=p}}.

達.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|辵|羍|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc|alt1=辶}}.

Note that inner component is not {{zh-l|*幸}} – these are not related, and there is an extra stroke in {{lang|zh|達}} (three horizontal strokes at the bottom, not two).

遡.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

The original character is [[㴑]]. {{Han compound|水|alt1=氵|㡿|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=water}}. It expresses the meaning “to go upstream” or “to go back.”
The commonly used modern character is based on a different form. {{Han compound|辵|alt1=辶|朔|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=movement}}.<ref>Digital Shinjigen 2017</ref>

遣.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
Originally {{zh-l|*𠳋}}, an {{liushu|ic|nocap=1}} in the {{w|oracle bone script}}: two {{zh-l|*又|hand}} in a configuration of commanding + {{zh-l|*𠂤|army}} – “to send; to dispatch”.

The {{zh-l|*辵}} component was later added in some {{w|bronze inscriptions}}, which is retained in the modern glyph.

選.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|止|巽|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=|t2=}}.

Xu Shen argues that the original meaning was to respectfully reject and send someone back; to choose also implies rejecting others, hence the meaning.<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref>

那.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|冉|alt1=冄|邑|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=city}}.

Originally the name of a state; later borrowed for a [[w:Demonstrative#Distal_and_proximal_demonstratives|distal demonstrative]].

邪.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|牙|邑|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=town; city}}.

The semantic component suggests that this character was originally used in a place name, which ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' suggests to be {{zh-l|*琅邪}}. It was later borrowed for other meanings, the most prominent being “wrong; evil” (originally written as {{zh-l|衺}}).

重.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|人|東|t1=man|t2=bag|ls=psc|ls2=ic|c1=s|c2=p}} – a man carrying a bag. A glyph {{zh-l|*土|earth}} was later added to show that the bag-carrying man is standing on the ground. From this composition ([[𡍺]]) is the current form. In the modern form, the man on top can be seen as [[亻]] (however, the upper part of the character is unrelated to [[千]]).

See also [[以]], in which a person is carrying something, [[央]], in which a person is carrying a pole, and [[年]], in which a person is carrying the harvest.

長.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – an old man with long hair; an ancient variant still used is [[镸]] (e.g., [[⾽]] and [[鬓]]).

Compare {{l-lite|zh|sc=Hani|彡}} and the top component of [[老]] and its variant [[考]].

開.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|門|一|廾|t1=door|t2=latch and/or wooden pole that shuts a door|t3=a pair of hands|ls=ic}} — a pair of hands opening a latched door. {{zh-l|*一}} and {{zh-l|*廾}} are combined into {{zh-l|*开}} since the Small Seal script; however, in the conservative variant [[𨵑]], the two hands and the latch are well visible.

Compare {{zh-l|*闢}}. See also {{zh-l|*關}}, in which the door is kept shut by two wooden poles.

陪.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|阜|咅|t1=soil mountain|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}
The {{m|zh|阜|tr=-}} suggests, by extension from the meaning of “pile up soil”, to join up with, to be close to: to accompany.<ref>{{R:Kojien}}</ref>

陶.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
In oracle bone and bronze inscriptions, {{Han compound|ls=ic|阜|勹|土|t1=mound of dirt|t2=two people|t3=earth|nocap=yes}} – "to dig", original character of {{zh-l|*掏}}. The shapes beneath the outermost {{zh-l|*勹}} were corrupted in {{w|bamboo slips}}, arriving at the modern form {{zh-l|*缶}} in seal script. 

Now a {{Han compound|ls=psc|ls2=ic|阜|匋|alt1=阝|c1=s|c2=p|t1=soil mountain|t2=make pottery|nocap=yes}}.

隻.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|隹|又|t1=short-tailed bird|t2=hand|ls=ic}}.

To hold one bird is {{lang|zh|隻}}; to hold two birds is {{zh-l|*雙}}.

集.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|隹|木|t1=bird|t2=tree|ls=ic}}.
The variant {{l|zh|雧}} shows three birds instead.

雨.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – a cloud with drops of rain falling from it. The horizontal stroke at the top was added later. (Compare {{noncog|egy|ḥwt|𓇲}}.)
{{wp|zh:,yue:,gan:,hak:yí,cdo:ṳ̄,nan:hō͘,wuu:}}

非.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} - two wings facing opposite directions. Originally, it represented wings; then, according to Xu Shen, its meaning changed into "leave; disobey" ([[違]]). Eventually, it was used perhaps as a phonetic loan to mean "not to be".

Unrelated to the Kangxi radical [[韭]].

面.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}}, A man’s face, with an eye exaggerated, derived from {{zh-l|*𦣻}}. The horizontal stroke was added later. Unrelated to {{zh-l|*自}}.

[[#Etymology 2|Etymology 2]]: {{Han simp|麵|f=麥|t=&nbsp;}} as adopted by the ''Chinese Character Simplification Scheme'' ({{lw|zh|pref=《|s:漢字簡化方案|漢字簡化方案|suf=》}}) in 1956.

頃.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|匕|頁|t2=head|ls=ic}}.

The head is askew, the original meaning of {{zh-m|傾}}.

順.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|川|頁|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}.
[[w:Shuowen Jiezi|''Shuowen'']] considers it to be an {{Han compound|川|t1=river|頁|t2=head|ls=ic|nocap=1}} - flowing in the river, i.e. following/obeying the river's current. Compare the English phrase {{m|en|go with the flow}}.

頑.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|元|頁|c1=p|c2=s|ls=psc|t1=head|t2=head}}.

As {{zh-m|元|tr=-}} came to be used to mean “original”, rather than “head”, this character was formed from the original meaning of {{zh-m|元|tr=-|t=head}} + the semantic element {{zh-m|頁|tr=-}} to affirm the “head” meaning, but with the modification in meaning to “an intransigent head”; the head of a heady person.

飛.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}}: a [[bird]] [[fly]]ing [[upwards]].

In its modern form, resembles a tasseled {{zh-l|升}}.

食.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|ls=ic|亼|皀|t1=mouth|t2=a bowl of rice on a stand}}. In the modern form, the bowl of rice has corrupted into the similar but unrelated {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|良}} or {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|艮}}. The variant form {{m-lite|zh|sc=Hani|𠊊}} preserves the original composition.

[[w:Shuowen Jiezi|''Shuowen'']] interprets the {{och-l|*亼}} as a phonetic component.

飽.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|食|包|c1=s|c2=p|t1=food|ls=psc}}.

For etymology 2, the {{zh-l|*食}} radical specifies its dietary use (instead of just being a generic wrap).

飾.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|飤|巾|t2=cloth|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}.

The {{zh-l|*人}} has changed shape into {{zh-l|*𠂉}}.

馬.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}

{{liushu|p}} – a horse with its head facing the left, showing a flowing mane in the wind, standing vertically on two legs.<ref>{{cite-web |en |url=https://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/nll/?p=72810 |title=Evolution of the sinograph and the word for horse |first=Victor |last=Mair |work={{w|Language Log}} |date=2026-02-16 |accessdate=2026-02-16 |publisher={{w|University of Pennsylvania}}}}</ref> In the bronze inscriptions, the head was often simplified into an eye ({{m|zh|目}}). The legs eventually evolved into four dots ({{m|zh|灬}}, unrelated to {{m|zh|火}}).

Contrast with {{m|zh|鹿|t=deer}}, which saw a very different development, and {{m|zh|𢊁}} (as in {{m|zh|薦}}), which is a hybrid: it has the legs of {{lang|zh|馬}} ({{m|zh|灬}}) but the head of {{m|zh|鹿}}.

鬼.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – a kneeling man or figure with an ugly face ([[甶]]). The ugly face could be a mask. The lower right “dot” is a small {{zh-l|*厶}}; it is a residual tail that was added later – compare {{zh-l|*离}}. Related to [[畏]] (variant [[𠂽]]), which originally depicts a ghost standing with a stick in hand.

Compare {{zh-l|*異|different, strange}}, in which the {{zh-l|*田}} also derives from a face and indicates a mask. Compare also [[禺]], in which the top component represents either a mask or a monkey's head.

鮮.originRaw:
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|魚|羴|c1=s|c2=p|abb2=y|t1=fish|ls=psc}}.

Alternatively, may be {{Han compound|魚|羊|t1=fish|t2=sheep|ls=ic|nocap=y}} – delicious.

鳥.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}}: a bird with a dangling tail.

Compare {{zh-l|*烏}}, which is very similar, of similar origin, and {{zh-l|*隹}}, which originated from a similar image of a bird, but is today rather more abstract and less recognizable. Compare to Egyptian [[𓄿]]

鹿.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|p}} – a deer. Current form is highly abstracted – legs have transformed to {{zh-l|*比}} – note grouping of front and rear legs, which are bent – head has transformed to middle component (similar to {{zh-l|*凸}}/{{zh-l|*曲}}), while antlers on top and extended lip/mouth on left transformed into {{zh-l|*广}}. This transformation occurred during seal characters.

Contrast the very different development of {{zh-l|*馬|horse}}, and the transformation in {{zh-l|*廌}} (as in {{zh-l|*薦}}), which has the head of {{lang|zh|鹿}} but the legs of {{lang|zh|馬}} ({{zh-l|*灬}}).

鼻.originRaw:
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|自|t1=nose|畀|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|no_och1=y}}.

{{och-l|*自}} originally referred to this word, but came to be used to refer to {{och-l|自|self}} instead, so the sense of “nose” has been replaced by this character. Some scholars interpret {{och-l|鼻*}} as a combination of a nose ({{och-l|*自}}) and two lungs ({{och-l|*畀}}), however {{lang|zh|sc=Hani|畀}} on oracle bones represents an arrow.
